WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:03.610
Hello, everyone.

00:00:03.610 --> 00:00:04.560
I'm Brindha Muniappan.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.600
I'm the Director of
Programs here at the museum

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:10.410
and I'm happy to welcome you
to the museum this evening.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:14.730
I'm also quite pleased to have
Eran Egozy and Jason Levine

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:15.630
here.

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.400
I'm sure you're
all eager as well

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:22.260
for a wonderful conversation
and performance this evening.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.620
Eran has probably been
thinking about bringing Jason

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.920
to MIT for a long time,
but it was about a year ago

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:34.740
when he and I talked about how
we might bring Jason in front

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.220
of the public to have a
conversation about what he does

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:39.780
and why and how.

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:43.800
And, from that conversation
developed tonight's program,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.830
as well as a coding
workshop that Jason

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:50.890
is going to lead on
Thursday here at the museum.

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:53.920
I think there are a couple of
spots left for that workshop

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.250
so, if you're interested
in joining us,

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.580
please look online at the same
place where you got your ticket

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:01.690
for this program tonight.

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:03.720
So, without further
ado, I'd like

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.450
to introduce Eran
Egozy, who is Professor

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:09.000
of the Practice of
Music Technology at MIT,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.850
and you might know him better
as co-founder of Harmonics.

00:01:14.483 --> 00:01:15.150
Thanks, Brindha.

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:19.100
Hey, everyone.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.410
Thanks for coming.

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:23.590
I'm really excited,
today, to be introducing

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:26.860
Jason to come talk to you
all and perform for you all.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.480
And then after
that, he and I will

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.022
have a little
discussion here and try

00:01:30.022 --> 00:01:31.810
to get you guys
involved as well.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:35.590
It's really awesome to
see students of my class.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.720
Raise your hand, students.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.610
Hey, awesome.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.450
Jason is actually here
for the whole week.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:44.080
And it's part of a residence
that we're having so, he's

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.360
giving this talk tonight
plus the workshop on Thursday

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:47.530
that Brindha talked about.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.840
Bu he's also going to
be lecturing in my class

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.870
tomorrow for my class,
Interactive Music Systems,

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:55.420
and will be talking to students
about their final projects

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.550
and giving them some
advice about that.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:02:01.060
So we're really looking
forward to him working with us

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:02.560
in that capacity.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.920
I met Jason, God we
were talking about this,

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.229
I think it was 2011.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:10.539
It was when I was still
working at Harmonics

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.360
and the Kinect
had just come out.

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.940
And there was a conference
down at Carnegie Mellon

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.500
called The Art and
Code Conference,

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.720
and they were particularly
looking at ways

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:22.900
to use code to make cool
art using the Kinect.

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:26.710
So I went down there,
as well as someone

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.820
I see here from
Harmonics, Mike Mandel.

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:35.260
And Jason was doing
this crazy performance

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.590
where he was on stage
with the screen behind him

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.840
and the Kinect was
doing a 3D capture

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.277
of his position and his body.

00:02:43.277 --> 00:02:45.110
And that was being
rendered onto the screen,

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:46.780
but using waveforms.

00:02:46.780 --> 00:02:50.170
And it also turns out he's one
of the world's top beatboxing

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.380
artists, so he was beatboxing
and doing this kind of hard

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.790
to describe show.

00:02:54.790 --> 00:02:57.460
And since then, he's just
done more and more interesting

00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:01.400
things and he'll talk
about all of those today.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.820
So, I won't bore you
with more introductions

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:05.320
other to say that,
again, I'm really

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:06.520
excited to have Jason here.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:07.600
Jason, come on up.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:19.030
Hello, Hello.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:21.670
So, my name is
Jason, as you know,

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.000
and I'm a computational
artist and musician.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:28.390
And I'm based in New York,
but I grew up in Montreal,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:31.150
so I really appreciate
the summer weather you

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.320
guys are having right now.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.430
So I've been interested in
music and computers for as long

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:40.930
as I can remember and my parents
took notice of my interest

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:42.290
in computers.

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:44.110
And so they went
to a garage sale

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.790
and picked up a used computer.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:48.880
So this was my first computer.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.430
It was the IBM PC junior, had
128 kilobytes of RAM and 4.7

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.130
megahertz CPU and no hard drive.

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:01.150
So, if you have no hard
drive or, any drive

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.000
at all, what happens when
you start up a computer?

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:07.420
Well, I would just
get this screen.

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:09.680
I didn't know what
to do with it.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.470
But, lucky for me, it
came with a manual.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.280
So I taught myself
Basic by writing

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.800
these choose your own adventure
style text adventures.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:22.360
So something like, you
see a door, open door.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.090
You enter a room with a
lamp in it, turn on lamp.

00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:27.790
You see a dresser with
a drawer, open drawer.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:30.520
There's a box in the
drawer, and on and on.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:35.350
So, I would spend all day trying
to build these text adventures

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:37.840
but then every night, my
father would come down

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.000
and because, in
these times there

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.870
was no sleep mode or
really even screen savers,

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.790
the computer had
to be turned off.

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.330
And since I had no hard
drive, everything was erased.

00:04:49.330 --> 00:04:51.580
So I had this zen-like
practice of trying

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:55.120
to take the text adventure
to the next level each day

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.810
until all my work was erased.

00:04:58.810 --> 00:05:03.400
And my father was talking with
some friends of my plight,

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:08.290
and so, one of his friends gave
him a 5 and 1/4 disk entitled

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:12.010
DOS 1.1, so I didn't
get the manual

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.230
but, I was allowed to
use it once in a while.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.370
So I still didn't
have a hard drive,

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:19.000
but I just basically left
this drive in my computer

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.020
all the time.

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.400
And there was no
games or much to do,

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:25.000
so I just learned how
to do batch files.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:31.510
And my one achievement was, I
edited the binary of the system

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:37.000
kernel by hand and I replaced
all instances of exe with fun,

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.610
and I swear to God it worked.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:44.440
Executable files would not run
until you renamed them .fun.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.090
I'm sure you can't
do that anymore,

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.760
but, you know, I
had a lot of time.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:55.060
So, I was clearly looking
for fun in computers

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.580
and I found it at
elementary school

00:05:57.580 --> 00:05:59.680
where they taught Logo Writer.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.390
So, for those of
you not familiar,

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:06.430
Logo Writer was a programming
language aimed for educators.

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.010
And you had this
little turtle and you

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:11.560
could draw a line by saying,
go forward 10 pixels,

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:15.850
turn right 90 degrees, and make
all sorts of abstract geometry.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.550
But, it was advanced
enough you could also make

00:06:18.550 --> 00:06:22.330
some animations or even a game.

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:29.910
So it turned out I really
liked it and I don't

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.820
remember most of this because
obviously it was 1992.

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:35.100
But, the moment
that I do remember,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.380
was when the three of
us were put in a room

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.180
and we were given five problems.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.220
And we had to pick
which three we

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:43.680
were going to solve in this
limited amount of time,

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:45.690
and then we went
to solving them.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.240
And I think it was
my first experience

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.780
of the sort of liveness that
can happen in programming.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:55.860
So around the same time
or maybe a bit earlier,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.100
another friend of my parents
invited me to perform with

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.610
the [? Musique ?] de
[? Moliére ?] Chamber Orchestra

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:06.600
doing a Mozart's
music for children.

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.070
And I had the cuckoo, which
is the one-holed flute.

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.310
And I had one job and that
was to remember the time when

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.597
the orchestra stops
and then I go,

00:07:16.597 --> 00:07:19.950
toot toot, toot toot, toot
toot toot toot toot toot.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.620
But, the story was,
they never knew

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.960
when I was going to come
in, and every time I

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:26.580
invented some new rhythm.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.640
And they had to be on their toes
to know when was the right time

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.720
to jump back in.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:34.230
So, basically by
age 11, I already

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:41.070
had a taste for liveness
in coding, hacking system

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.080
critical files without
making a backup

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:46.320
and wild and unruly
musical improvisations.

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:51.540
And now 25 years
later, I'm taller.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:57.450
So, I did learn spreadsheets,
databases, touch typing and C

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:00.210
in high school, but
really most of my energy

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.100
was focused on this
new high energy,

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.620
I see you guys are
all like, who are

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:09.360
those bands, this kind
of high energy rock music

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:10.710
that was coming out.

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.520
And I liked it so much that
when I finished high school,

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.740
I started a band
with some friends

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:19.260
and ended up dropping
out of science

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.910
and going to music school.

00:08:20.910 --> 00:08:23.190
I was never much
of a bass player,

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.720
but one thing that
I really loved

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.080
was all the pedals, the delays,
the distortions, the reverbs,

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.120
the flanges, the phasors.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:32.309
These little boxes with
knobs that you could just

00:08:32.309 --> 00:08:34.590
control and shape the sound.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.820
But, unlike my guitarist
friends at the time who

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:38.789
were collecting the
little Boss pedals,

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.909
I went on a little-known
site called, eBay,

00:08:41.909 --> 00:08:45.000
and got a programmable
multi-effects pedal.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:47.520
So, the signal chain
was fixed, but you

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:50.730
could change which effects
were on and the parameters.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.295
It was very awkward
to program, you

00:08:52.295 --> 00:08:53.670
see you have these
little buttons

00:08:53.670 --> 00:08:56.310
to move around and change stuff.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:59.850
But my best friend,
Diego, realized

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.110
that you could push the
delay to 3 and 1/2 seconds

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:08.020
and you could set it so when
you pushed the foot pedal up,

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:10.380
it set the feedback
of the delay loop

00:09:10.380 --> 00:09:13.690
to 100% making it
into a loop pedal.

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:17.100
Now, I know today, live
looping is practically

00:09:17.100 --> 00:09:18.930
a musical genre of
its own and there is

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:21.090
plenty of dedicated hardware.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.420
But, back in 2000-2001,
there was nothing.

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:28.230
Maybe just one rack mount
unit from TC Helicon

00:09:28.230 --> 00:09:31.590
that cost a couple grand.

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.250
So, Diego got one
of these pedals,

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.340
and we started a group
called Tabula Rasa.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.140
And I played all sorts of
percussion, guitar, beatboxing

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:44.340
and throat singing.

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.762
And he played bass,
flute and singing,

00:09:46.762 --> 00:09:48.970
but the truth was, we were
really playing the pedals.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:51.390
So we had these
choreographies where

00:09:51.390 --> 00:09:53.640
we would change effects
at the same time

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:57.990
to create these song structures
and polyrhythmic effects.

00:09:57.990 --> 00:10:02.100
But I felt like I needed even
more programmatic control.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:07.860
So we got this 26-channel
mixer and connected everything

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:09.930
we possibly could
to it and set it up

00:10:09.930 --> 00:10:13.477
so you could send
any signal anywhere.

00:10:13.477 --> 00:10:15.060
And even send the
pedals to each other

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:18.000
and create these wild
feedback effects.

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.500
So we did that for
a couple of years

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.400
but ultimately, it was too
complicated and unwieldy

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:25.270
to perform with live.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:28.860
And, so, we created a more
stripped down live band.

00:10:28.860 --> 00:10:32.200
After music school, I went
to do computer science.

00:10:32.200 --> 00:10:37.530
But, again, I spent
more time beatboxing

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:40.230
than actually going to school.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.460
I beatboxed in bars and clubs
and weird circus festivals

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:45.250
in the forest.

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:48.120
And then I would put
on a shirt and beatbox

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.700
in a wholesome a capella group.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:52.980
But I just couldn't get
down with the way computer

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:53.940
science was taught.

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.130
There wasn't much
coding, to be honest.

00:10:56.130 --> 00:11:00.030
And it was just very dry,
static and theoretical.

00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:02.760
Until halfway
through my degree I

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:06.150
found out that I could do a
minor in music technology.

00:11:06.150 --> 00:11:09.780
And this is where I learned
about physical computing

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:12.240
and, especially, Max MSP.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:15.160
So this was just an
absolute game changer.

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:20.640
It solved, wait, raise of hands
if you've heard of Max MSP,

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:23.310
yeah, that's what I thought.

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:25.380
This is just a
game changer coming

00:11:25.380 --> 00:11:27.930
from the kind of hardware
system with this mess of cables

00:11:27.930 --> 00:11:31.380
because now, I could set up my
signal chain however I wanted,

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.420
I could have as many
delays as I want,

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:35.820
loops as long as I wanted.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:38.790
But even more than that,
there was this notion

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.790
of interactivity that you
could take a sensor to control

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:46.110
parameters or, my
favorite was using

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:48.870
the signal of a performer
to actually affect

00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:50.610
their own signal.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.060
An example of that.

00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.550
This is a performance.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.030
So, whenever the singer holds
a note for three seconds,

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:05.330
it captures the eight loudest
harmonics in her voice

00:12:05.330 --> 00:12:08.420
and tunes some
resonant filters, which

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.775
I'm then beatboxing through.

00:12:10.775 --> 00:12:11.900
And then we're improvising.

00:12:11.900 --> 00:12:14.226
[BEATBOXING]

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.710
But as much as I love
music technology,

00:12:36.710 --> 00:12:40.232
I felt so burnt out from
the computer science degree.

00:12:40.232 --> 00:12:41.690
And in the end, I
decided, you know

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.360
what, I think I'm going to
follow the path of performance.

00:12:45.360 --> 00:12:50.850
And I literally ran away
and joined the circus.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:54.990
So, I played, most
notably, I was

00:12:54.990 --> 00:12:59.520
the beatboxer emcee in a Kung
Fu themed street circus group.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.580
But, I also battled
flamenco dancers,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.730
accompanied DJs, and this
picture in the corner

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:07.690
here, that's not
even a circus group.

00:13:07.690 --> 00:13:11.080
Those are my
roommates at the time.

00:13:11.080 --> 00:13:14.110
And so, I literally thought
this would be my life.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:18.360
I'd travel the world just
beatboxing and repeating

00:13:18.360 --> 00:13:20.790
silly comedy lines.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.430
But then one by one all
my circus collaborators

00:13:23.430 --> 00:13:27.580
started studying
contemporary dance.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:33.340
And I remember, when my
closest collaborator [? Manu ?]

00:13:33.340 --> 00:13:38.290
[INAUDIBLE] asked me to vocally
accompany and be the soundtrack

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:39.940
for his first choreography.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:41.950
And we were in this
acoustically dry room

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:46.270
and I was trying to do my
explosive sound effects,

00:13:46.270 --> 00:13:49.180
but he was doing all these
subtle and bizarre movements

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:51.290
and it just didn't work.

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:53.260
So I knew exactly
what I had to do.

00:13:53.260 --> 00:13:57.190
I dug up some old Max patches
and I created a system

00:13:57.190 --> 00:13:59.770
with this very long
delay that was slowly

00:13:59.770 --> 00:14:01.390
increasing in
feedback, so that it

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:05.410
began with very
one-to-one sound effects,

00:14:05.410 --> 00:14:10.840
but slowly became a
layered soundscape.

00:14:14.493 --> 00:14:16.457
[WHOOSHING]

00:14:16.457 --> 00:14:18.920
[SOUNDSCAPE PLAYING]

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:55.410
So, I continued to work
with the circus groups

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:57.810
but also start
incorporating more

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:01.440
of these interactive
audio systems

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.330
into the work with performers.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:06.870
But what I started to feel was
that these complex and subtle

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:11.490
interactions and sounds
needed to be visualized.

00:15:11.490 --> 00:15:16.680
I did a little bit of work
visualizing with Max MSP.

00:15:16.680 --> 00:15:20.550
But then, someone told
me about something called

00:15:20.550 --> 00:15:22.500
openFrameworks.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:25.320
I see a smile over here.

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:29.130
So openFrameworks, when I first
heard of openFrameworks I said,

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:32.460
Oh man, I haven't done C++
in like five years since I

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:35.400
finished my degree, I don't
know if I remember it anymore.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:37.200
But it only took
me one time to know

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:39.840
that this would be
my tool of choice

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:43.800
because, unlike the
black boxes of Max MSP,

00:15:43.800 --> 00:15:47.940
I could have direct manipulation
of data at the lowest levels.

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:52.410
And just have high performance
graphics and an easy interface

00:15:52.410 --> 00:15:57.000
to work with all sorts of
media from video, to images,

00:15:57.000 --> 00:16:00.190
to graphics, to sound.

00:16:00.190 --> 00:16:03.300
And so I started making visuals
with openFrameworks but then

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.770
again I felt like
I wish there was

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.830
a way that the
visuals could be aware

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:09.880
of the physical performers.

00:16:09.880 --> 00:16:13.620
So I started doing some
research into IR sensors

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:16.020
and I dreamt up this
whole system that

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:18.090
was going to cost
me one or two grand

00:16:18.090 --> 00:16:22.200
and it would have been
super complicated to set up,

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:23.640
and hard to use.

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:26.070
And, you've probably
guessed the punch line,

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:31.320
because around then, Microsoft
released the Xbox for Kinect.

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:34.140
Sorry, Kinect for Xbox.

00:16:34.140 --> 00:16:39.150
And as soon as it was released,
Adafruit put a bounty on it

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:41.400
for a couple of grand, whoever
could hack the drivers.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:45.720
So, within one month,
there was full support

00:16:45.720 --> 00:16:48.180
for the Kinect in
openFrameworks.

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:54.120
So I set to creating interactive
video pieces with dancers.

00:16:54.120 --> 00:16:56.640
So this is a piece
with video feedback

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:00.150
and the angle and
scale of the projection

00:17:00.150 --> 00:17:03.570
is controlled by
where the dancer is.

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:06.779
This is tracking the vectors
of the movement of the dancer.

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:16.920
And then in this one, it's
a particle system reacting

00:17:16.920 --> 00:17:18.569
to the dancer's movements.

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.790
But what you can't see
or, maybe can't see,

00:17:20.790 --> 00:17:22.390
is that these are all letters.

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.270
And they're the
letters of a poem.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:28.359
And the piece begins with
him reciting the poem.

00:17:28.359 --> 00:17:31.350
And as he starts to
move away, the letters

00:17:31.350 --> 00:17:33.690
all start to follow
him like alphabet soup.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:35.460
And the way we created
this piece was,

00:17:35.460 --> 00:17:38.040
I had a sort of basic
particle system,

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.340
and then he would dance with it
and then give me suggestions.

00:17:41.340 --> 00:17:45.750
Can it start from this and
these letters leave first.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:49.790
Maybe it moves along
this type of curve.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:52.080
And so, every time you
give me a suggestion,

00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:55.410
I would quit the system and
then I'd start typing like mad

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:59.100
and try and turn the idea into
reality, start up the app again

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.450
and then we'd
iterate in that way.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:04.840
And so, I didn't know about
live coding at the time,

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:06.930
but that's what I
was trying to do.

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:09.390
The only problem was that
I had to quit the app

00:18:09.390 --> 00:18:12.630
and then the dancer couldn't
interact with the system

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.700
while I was making
changes to it.

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:21.970
So, I refined the technique.

00:18:21.970 --> 00:18:25.370
And this is with a dance company
in New York called Eric Taylor

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:25.870
Dance.

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:53.040
And, this led to me working with
the legendary Miss Lauryn Hill.

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:56.410
I got to do audio
reactive visuals for her.

00:18:56.410 --> 00:18:59.380
And it was great
because the entire band,

00:18:59.380 --> 00:19:01.600
I got an audio signal from
every member of the band

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:03.670
and then I could
choose which visuals

00:19:03.670 --> 00:19:05.440
and what part of
the projection were

00:19:05.440 --> 00:19:10.430
reacting to her voice or
the drums or guitar solo.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:11.125
Here's the--

00:19:11.125 --> 00:19:15.438
LAURYN HILL: (SINGING) I
treat this like my thesis.

00:19:15.438 --> 00:19:17.230
Well-written topic
broken down into pieces.

00:19:17.230 --> 00:19:18.280
I introduce then produce.

00:19:18.280 --> 00:19:19.235
Words so profuse.

00:19:19.235 --> 00:19:21.360
It's abuse how I juice up
this beat like I'm deuce.

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:23.527
Two people both equal Like
I'm Gemini rather simeon.

00:19:23.527 --> 00:19:25.540
If I Jimmy on this
lock I could pop it.

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:26.360
You can't stop it.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:26.780
Drop it.

00:19:26.780 --> 00:19:28.030
Your whole crew's microscopic.

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:30.230
Like particles while I make
international articles.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:30.716
And on the cover.

00:19:30.716 --> 00:19:32.174
Don't discuss the
baby mother business.

00:19:32.174 --> 00:19:34.118
I been in this third LP you
can't tell me, I witness.

00:19:34.118 --> 00:19:34.604
First handed.

00:19:34.604 --> 00:19:35.090
I'm candid.

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:35.576
You can't stand it.

00:19:35.576 --> 00:19:36.560
Respect demanded.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:37.230
And get flown around the planet.

00:19:37.230 --> 00:19:38.830
Rock hard like granite or steel.

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.600
People feel Miss Hill
from Brooklyn to Israel.

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:42.580
And this is real.

00:19:42.580 --> 00:19:43.540
So I keep makin' the
street's ballads.

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.420
While you lookin' for
dressin' to go with--

00:19:45.420 --> 00:19:46.420
You could get the money.

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:48.820
You could get the power--

00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:53.460
So just one geeky aside, if
there's any signal enthusiasts

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:54.570
out there.

00:19:54.570 --> 00:19:59.130
Lauryn Hill's voice is a
really incredible signal.

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:00.380
It's just different.

00:20:00.380 --> 00:20:03.060
She's legendary.

00:20:03.060 --> 00:20:05.350
But let's go back in time.

00:20:05.350 --> 00:20:07.290
The first piece I
actually created

00:20:07.290 --> 00:20:10.110
when I first got the
Kinect and openFrameworks

00:20:10.110 --> 00:20:12.270
working together,
was a solo piece

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:18.090
that was a synthesis of my work
in physical theater, dance,

00:20:18.090 --> 00:20:21.720
beatboxing and
interactive video.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:23.727
And that was called [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:28.980 --> 00:20:31.689
And this is the piece
that Eran was mentioning.

00:20:31.689 --> 00:20:34.184
[SOUND EFFECTS]

00:20:59.140 --> 00:21:01.660
And back in the day when I'd
send this video to people,

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:03.580
it was impossible to
convince them that there

00:21:03.580 --> 00:21:05.020
was no post-processing.

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:07.012
[BEATBOXING]

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:37.580
So I got to take the
performance on the tour.

00:21:37.580 --> 00:21:40.730
And the first stop
was Art &amp;&amp; Code 3D,

00:21:40.730 --> 00:21:44.630
which was the Kinect Hacking
Conference put on by Golan

00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:49.010
Levin in the STUDIO for Creative
Inquiry at Carnegie Mellon.

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:53.630
And something amazing happened
during my performance there.

00:21:53.630 --> 00:21:58.700
And, well, not amazing but,
that is that the code crashed.

00:21:58.700 --> 00:22:01.040
And it crashed
right in the middle.

00:22:01.040 --> 00:22:03.200
So if it had crashed
at the beginning,

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:06.080
I would have asked the
audience, can I start again.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:07.755
And if it had
crashed near the end,

00:22:07.755 --> 00:22:09.380
I would have just
pretended that that's

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:10.790
where it was supposed to end.

00:22:10.790 --> 00:22:13.160
But it crashed
right in the middle

00:22:13.160 --> 00:22:16.640
and it actually crashed to the
point where the visuals go dark

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:19.160
and it's just ambient
right before the build up

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:21.330
to the final climax.

00:22:21.330 --> 00:22:25.340
So I just, jumped off
stage, I forced quit the app

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:29.180
and I just started debugging
in front of the whole audience.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:31.140
So I commented out
some code here,

00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:32.540
I changed some numbers there.

00:22:32.540 --> 00:22:33.530
It was a mad dash.

00:22:33.530 --> 00:22:35.120
I wasn't sure if it
was going to work.

00:22:35.120 --> 00:22:40.280
I hit compile and we all watched
as the rainbow beach ball went,

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:41.420
and it compiled.

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:45.560
And I jumped up on stage,
now full of adrenaline,

00:22:45.560 --> 00:22:47.600
and I finished the show.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:51.290
And the amazing part
was, multiple times

00:22:51.290 --> 00:22:53.563
throughout the weekend
of that conference,

00:22:53.563 --> 00:22:55.230
people came up to me
and they were like,

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.810
hey man, your show
is pretty cool,

00:22:56.810 --> 00:23:00.050
but that part where it crashed
and then you debugged it

00:23:00.050 --> 00:23:02.810
and then it worked,
that made my weekend.

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:05.150
I was like, really?

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:07.130
And I was so curious
but, it sort of

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:11.390
planted the seed that,
Oh, can programming

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.580
be a performance practice?

00:23:13.580 --> 00:23:17.180
Anyway, when I
first got off stage,

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:18.620
this unassuming
guy came up to me

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:21.650
and he said, hey, you probably
wouldn't be interested

00:23:21.650 --> 00:23:23.720
but, I have a
video game company,

00:23:23.720 --> 00:23:25.160
maybe we can work together.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:30.440
He gave me a card and it said,
Eran Egozy, CTO of Harmonics.

00:23:30.440 --> 00:23:32.960
So the next stop on
the tour was Harmonics,

00:23:32.960 --> 00:23:35.840
where I got to play
for the entire company

00:23:35.840 --> 00:23:39.200
during their lunch
hour, which was a really

00:23:39.200 --> 00:23:40.990
interesting experience.

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:44.060
Then, I went to Columbia to
play the International Image

00:23:44.060 --> 00:23:46.550
Festival, to India
to the Carnival

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:49.550
for e-Creativity, and
then finally to Norway

00:23:49.550 --> 00:23:51.200
for the Piksel
Festival, which is

00:23:51.200 --> 00:23:55.460
a really interesting
open-source software art

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:57.680
conference festival.

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:02.540
And at Piksel, there was all
these amazing and very strange

00:24:02.540 --> 00:24:05.420
performances, all with Linux.

00:24:05.420 --> 00:24:10.700
And one of them was this guy
writing code in an Emacs buffer

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:13.395
and creating these groovy beats.

00:24:13.395 --> 00:24:15.020
And after the show
I said, hey man what

00:24:15.020 --> 00:24:16.580
was that, that was incredible.

00:24:16.580 --> 00:24:19.250
And he said, Oh it's just
a [? haska ?] library

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.290
I wrote, and then walked away.

00:24:21.290 --> 00:24:24.800
I guess he was in a rush because
I didn't even catch his name.

00:24:24.800 --> 00:24:31.730
But that was Alex McClain, who
is a pioneer in the live coding

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.760
movement and the creator
of Title Cycles, which

00:24:34.760 --> 00:24:39.350
is used a lot at what are called
algoraves, where people dance

00:24:39.350 --> 00:24:41.315
to algorithmically
created music.

00:24:44.540 --> 00:24:47.210
After the tour I
went to Harmonics

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:50.510
to work on this incredible
project called Fantasia:

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:56.030
Music Evolved, which was to
take the classic 1940s Disney

00:24:56.030 --> 00:24:59.840
movie, Fantasia, and make
it an interactive game where

00:24:59.840 --> 00:25:02.260
you could manipulate music.

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:06.890
And so, they hired me to come
in and I couldn't believe it.

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:10.090
They said, we want you to
just make cool things where

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:12.490
you manipulate music
using the Kinect

00:25:12.490 --> 00:25:14.920
and there's cool visuals.

00:25:14.920 --> 00:25:16.120
Are you serious?

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.190
OK.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:22.240
So, here's a little
example of the game.

00:25:25.180 --> 00:25:27.830
Now, if you haven't tried
the game, you should try it.

00:25:27.830 --> 00:25:29.710
It's extremely expensive.

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:31.360
I can't possibly show--

00:25:31.360 --> 00:25:34.006
[MUSIC- LORDE, "ROYALS"]

00:25:34.006 --> 00:25:37.750
(SINGING) [INAUDIBLE]

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:55.570
So this was one of the
things that I contributed

00:25:55.570 --> 00:25:59.740
to the game, where the user can
manipulate this sort of womp

00:25:59.740 --> 00:26:03.310
bass in a sound or some other--

00:26:03.310 --> 00:26:04.670
as it were sort of a late--

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:05.920
I know it was pretty abstract.

00:26:09.730 --> 00:26:14.170
And one memory I
have at Harmonics,

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:17.788
so I did about three
contracts with Harmonics.

00:26:17.788 --> 00:26:19.330
And at the end of
one of them I don't

00:26:19.330 --> 00:26:21.010
know if it was the
first or the second,

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:23.890
but I sort of got
this vibe like I

00:26:23.890 --> 00:26:27.430
needed to make another
good idea if I was

00:26:27.430 --> 00:26:29.330
going to get another contract.

00:26:29.330 --> 00:26:32.830
And so, I just started
hustling and I ended up

00:26:32.830 --> 00:26:36.010
making five different
interactive prototypes

00:26:36.010 --> 00:26:38.170
in five days.

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:42.013
I remember on the Friday Eran
and the other execs came in

00:26:42.013 --> 00:26:43.930
and they kind of looked
at my stuff they said,

00:26:43.930 --> 00:26:45.910
Oh that's cool, you
know, that doesn't work,

00:26:45.910 --> 00:26:47.810
maybe this could work with that.

00:26:47.810 --> 00:26:51.310
And then after they left,
Eran said, good work, man, I

00:26:51.310 --> 00:26:53.305
like this one
prototype a day thing.

00:26:53.305 --> 00:26:56.680
And I went, Oh no, oh no.

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:59.340
I set this precedent.

00:26:59.340 --> 00:27:01.180
But then, I thought
about it, and I

00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:04.870
was like no, this is
the most amazing thing

00:27:04.870 --> 00:27:07.420
that I love about this medium.

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:12.520
That you can have an idea for an
interactive audio-visual system

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:14.290
and create it in a
day and then people

00:27:14.290 --> 00:27:18.460
can interact with it by
moving their hands in the air.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:22.540
And that is the
magic that I've been

00:27:22.540 --> 00:27:26.420
after throughout my career.

00:27:26.420 --> 00:27:33.160
So, I got to work creating
interactive gestural control

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:34.960
instruments again.

00:27:34.960 --> 00:27:38.470
This, recently, with a
company called the WaveVR.

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:44.200
It's a social VR world where
people go to concerts together.

00:27:44.200 --> 00:27:50.570
So this is from the point of
view of the person who's in VR

00:27:50.570 --> 00:27:53.320
and they're remixing,
I should say,

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:59.830
this is LA producer, first-ever
album released in VR.

00:27:59.830 --> 00:28:04.740
So the team took her
album art and turned it

00:28:04.740 --> 00:28:07.170
into an entire world that
people could explore.

00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:11.520
This is remixing drum
samples from the album.

00:28:11.520 --> 00:28:13.508
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:28:36.910 --> 00:28:39.220
And now in this one
each globe represents

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:41.110
one of the stems,
one of the tracks,

00:28:41.110 --> 00:28:43.960
so the user's holding the drums.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:45.543
And then these are
different filters

00:28:45.543 --> 00:28:46.710
that they can put them into.

00:28:46.710 --> 00:28:49.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:29:00.712 --> 00:29:01.670
This is the keys track.

00:29:05.880 --> 00:29:19.130
[INAUDIBLE] So VR, in a way,
is a successor to the Kinect

00:29:19.130 --> 00:29:23.030
in that it's this
incredibly precise way

00:29:23.030 --> 00:29:25.820
to do gestural control.

00:29:25.820 --> 00:29:28.190
Of course, you've got to
hold things in your hand

00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:31.970
and wear goggles on your face.

00:29:31.970 --> 00:29:37.370
But, so, this is actually about
five years after Harmonics.

00:29:37.370 --> 00:29:41.330
But right after Harmonics, I
was accepted into the School

00:29:41.330 --> 00:29:52.420
for Poetic Computation, which
is a project of Taeyoon Choi

00:29:52.420 --> 00:29:55.550
and Zach Lieberman
and many others.

00:29:55.550 --> 00:29:58.360
And so this was the
first class in 2013.

00:29:58.360 --> 00:30:01.180
It was a really
incredible program.

00:30:01.180 --> 00:30:06.250
It was just this deprogramming
kind of education

00:30:06.250 --> 00:30:08.920
where you jumped
from topic to topic.

00:30:08.920 --> 00:30:11.770
Sometimes the students
talk the teachers,

00:30:11.770 --> 00:30:17.110
the class content changed
within one lesson.

00:30:17.110 --> 00:30:20.740
And the first
assignment Zach ever

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:26.890
gave was really amazing because
he said, find a programming

00:30:26.890 --> 00:30:30.550
language you've never heard
of or at least, never used,

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:33.520
and just do something
with it, anything.

00:30:33.520 --> 00:30:35.440
And so I kind of went
down this rabbit hole

00:30:35.440 --> 00:30:37.570
of trying all these
new languages.

00:30:37.570 --> 00:30:41.860
And that's where I found
out about live coding.

00:30:41.860 --> 00:30:43.990
Now I had heard
some live coding,

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:50.380
this sort of blips and noise
and complex rhythmic stuff.

00:30:50.380 --> 00:30:52.420
But this was totally different.

00:30:52.420 --> 00:30:57.160
This is some code from a
language called Impromptu

00:30:57.160 --> 00:31:00.200
created by Andrew Sorenson.

00:31:00.200 --> 00:31:03.920
And, this is called
A Study in Keith.

00:31:03.920 --> 00:31:10.040
And, it's inspired by the
Jazz pianist, Keith Jarrett.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:13.070
And the first 2 minutes
are silent while he sets up

00:31:13.070 --> 00:31:16.575
the base algorithms for
the structure of the piece.

00:31:16.575 --> 00:31:18.950
And I love this because it's
kind of got like a John Cage

00:31:18.950 --> 00:31:20.270
feel, but it's functional.

00:31:23.870 --> 00:31:25.660
But I'll skip ahead here.

00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:34.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:37.350
So this is so
different from what

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:39.880
you think of when you
think of algorithmic music.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:44.640
And if you look right over
here, what he's typing,

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:48.270
this is a super
minimal Markov chain.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:52.140
So Markov chain is usually
seen as a black box

00:31:52.140 --> 00:31:54.510
and you feed it tons
of data and then you

00:31:54.510 --> 00:31:56.220
use it to predict
the next turn based

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:57.880
on the last couple of terms.

00:31:57.880 --> 00:32:00.270
But, here, he's
writing it from scratch

00:32:00.270 --> 00:32:03.660
and then adding in to
create chord progressions.

00:32:03.660 --> 00:32:08.190
And, so, I love this idea of
sort of immediate programming

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:13.305
and it's almost artisanal in the
data and the code is together.

00:32:13.305 --> 00:32:25.780
[PIANO PLAYING]

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.330
So this really was
the moment for me

00:32:28.330 --> 00:32:32.860
when I realized that coding
would be my performance

00:32:32.860 --> 00:32:41.140
practice, because before
when I was bringing code

00:32:41.140 --> 00:32:45.610
and performance together, really
I would spend a couple of weeks

00:32:45.610 --> 00:32:48.220
head down creating
an interactive system

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:50.890
and then I'd spend a
couple of weeks practicing

00:32:50.890 --> 00:32:52.160
with the system.

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:55.340
So it was still
separated in time.

00:32:55.340 --> 00:32:57.760
And there's a great paper
called Programming in Time

00:32:57.760 --> 00:33:00.070
by Andrew Sorensen
and Henry Gardner,

00:33:00.070 --> 00:33:03.850
talking about how,
traditionally, programming

00:33:03.850 --> 00:33:09.070
has been split into a
problem to be solved,

00:33:09.070 --> 00:33:11.380
its implementation
and then a product

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:12.950
to be used in the future.

00:33:12.950 --> 00:33:17.140
And this software
development ideology

00:33:17.140 --> 00:33:20.230
is just how everyone
thinks about programming.

00:33:20.230 --> 00:33:23.410
But, it's nothing to
do with the hardware.

00:33:23.410 --> 00:33:25.190
It doesn't have to be this way.

00:33:25.190 --> 00:33:31.270
And, so, he talked about
this more ephemeral kind

00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:34.250
of programming where
it's direct agency.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:37.870
And you have ideas, you
turn them into code,

00:33:37.870 --> 00:33:40.900
and in the moment, you start
changing the sound waves,

00:33:40.900 --> 00:33:42.550
or, the photons in the room.

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:50.680
And so, I started to look
for like-minded souls.

00:33:50.680 --> 00:33:53.710
And it turns out
it's a very spread

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:55.400
out international community.

00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:59.770
There's a little hub in England,
in Mexico City, in Australia.

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:02.200
But not too many
people in New York.

00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:07.450
So, we started a group
with just four of us.

00:34:07.450 --> 00:34:12.219
So there's Kate Sicchio,
who live codes dancers.

00:34:12.219 --> 00:34:15.130
So this is sort of following
in the Merce Cunningham

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:20.080
games of chance sort of idea,
where she's algorithmically

00:34:20.080 --> 00:34:24.730
creating choreographies that
the dancers are reacting to

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:26.440
in real time.

00:34:26.440 --> 00:34:28.330
And then there's
Ramsey Nasser, who's

00:34:28.330 --> 00:34:32.409
created his own system for
live coding video games.

00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:34.270
So, while someone's
playing the game,

00:34:34.270 --> 00:34:37.840
he can change the
mechanics of the game.

00:34:37.840 --> 00:34:40.810
And then there's Tom
Murphy and myself,

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:45.340
who were traditional live
coders, live coding dance

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:47.739
music and such.

00:34:47.739 --> 00:34:53.889
And so, we're now about 10 core
members over 80 total members

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:58.480
and we've put on about 15
algoraves and two live coding

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:01.780
festivals, and we'd
love to do one here.

00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:10.180
So I want to talk
about how different

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:14.890
it is as a as a
musician to live code

00:35:14.890 --> 00:35:20.380
than just when you're
a human musician.

00:35:20.380 --> 00:35:24.400
And so as a musician, you're
always sort of in the moment.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.730
You've got this muscle memory,
your body is the instrument,

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:32.230
and you have all these
internalized ideas

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:35.320
of musical structures
and culture.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:37.450
And so you're living
in the moment,

00:35:37.450 --> 00:35:39.970
you have a little bit of the
past and a little bit of what

00:35:39.970 --> 00:35:41.600
could happen in the future.

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:46.780
And you can be very
fast and emotional.

00:35:46.780 --> 00:35:48.610
But when you're live
coding, the process

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:51.070
is completely different.

00:35:51.070 --> 00:35:55.480
You're in your computer,
you hear the music,

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:57.640
you think of how you
want to change it

00:35:57.640 --> 00:36:00.580
and then you imagine how to
turn that into an algorithm.

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:02.440
And then how to
type that in code

00:36:02.440 --> 00:36:06.160
fast enough so that when
you evaluate the change,

00:36:06.160 --> 00:36:07.960
it makes musical sense.

00:36:07.960 --> 00:36:10.630
And if you listen to a
lot of live coding music

00:36:10.630 --> 00:36:12.310
and you have a
musical background,

00:36:12.310 --> 00:36:14.380
you'll notice that
most of it doesn't

00:36:14.380 --> 00:36:19.390
follow the norms of
musical structure at all,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:21.340
and that's why.

00:36:21.340 --> 00:36:24.140
But I really wanted to
create something musical.

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:29.350
And so, I formed a duo with
singer-songwriter Mae Cheung.

00:36:29.350 --> 00:36:33.700
And she has a background in jazz
and she's a strong improviser.

00:36:33.700 --> 00:36:37.118
And what I realized was, when
I'm thinking about what to do,

00:36:37.118 --> 00:36:39.160
while I'm thinking how to
turn into an algorithm,

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:43.570
while I'm typing, I
still hear her voice

00:36:43.570 --> 00:36:46.570
in the corner of my ear.

00:36:46.570 --> 00:36:49.390
And her phrasing
guides me to know

00:36:49.390 --> 00:36:52.480
when to make a change to the
music, went to go half time,

00:36:52.480 --> 00:36:55.000
when to change the key.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:58.990
And so here's an example of
some of the music we make.

00:37:19.830 --> 00:37:20.710
People are confused.

00:37:29.930 --> 00:37:31.590
And we just have
these moments where

00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:33.150
we change at the same time.

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:48.560
Yeah and we've been lucky to
play all over New York, LA,

00:37:48.560 --> 00:37:51.050
touring in Mexico and Hong Kong.

00:37:51.050 --> 00:37:56.360
And just being greeted with
appreciation for this strange,

00:37:56.360 --> 00:37:59.530
sort of improvisational
electronic music that we do.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:04.840
People wear sunglasses to shows.

00:38:04.840 --> 00:38:07.792
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:38:35.870 --> 00:38:38.900
So I want to take
the next little bit

00:38:38.900 --> 00:38:41.420
to explain my
process and how I've

00:38:41.420 --> 00:38:45.350
come to the audio visual system
that I'm going to be presenting

00:38:45.350 --> 00:38:47.910
you this evening.

00:38:47.910 --> 00:38:50.510
So just to give you an
idea of the basic way

00:38:50.510 --> 00:38:55.340
you make rhythms
and live coding is

00:38:55.340 --> 00:38:58.860
it's a succession of
repeated conditionals.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:03.126
So you might say, every
fourth beat, play a kick drum.

00:39:03.126 --> 00:39:08.190
[BEATBOXING] And then on the
third beat of every four beats,

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:10.539
do a snare drum.

00:39:10.539 --> 00:39:16.592
[BEATBOXING] Play two
high hats every beat.

00:39:16.592 --> 00:39:25.230
[BEATBOXING] So, that's kind
of very direct and one-to-one.

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:28.740
But I wanted to find a
way to go one level higher

00:39:28.740 --> 00:39:32.190
and get more emergent rhythms.

00:39:32.190 --> 00:39:35.370
So what I started doing
was putting low frequency

00:39:35.370 --> 00:39:38.700
oscillators, sine
waves and such,

00:39:38.700 --> 00:39:42.540
and sampling them to choose
from a list of drums.

00:39:42.540 --> 00:39:45.930
So here's a simple example
with just three drums.

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:52.590
You'll see as I change
the frequency of the drum,

00:39:52.590 --> 00:39:55.050
you get some
interesting rhythms.

00:39:55.050 --> 00:39:57.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:40:11.850 --> 00:40:16.500
So, I started to push
this and I had 10 drums

00:40:16.500 --> 00:40:20.220
that I sorted by hand from
lowest to highest pitch, 20,

00:40:20.220 --> 00:40:21.100
30.

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:22.120
But I wanted more.

00:40:22.120 --> 00:40:26.850
I wanted like, 100
or 1,000 drums.

00:40:26.850 --> 00:40:29.270
And I knew I couldn't
have them all in one line.

00:40:29.270 --> 00:40:33.060
So I had to find a way
to have more dimensions.

00:40:33.060 --> 00:40:35.850
And right around
the time when I was

00:40:35.850 --> 00:40:38.730
trying to figure
out this problem,

00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:42.000
this image turned
up on the internet,

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:44.340
circulating Reddit
with the tagline,

00:40:44.340 --> 00:40:47.850
a computer made
this art by itself.

00:40:47.850 --> 00:40:51.810
Spoiler alert, computer
didn't make that art.

00:40:51.810 --> 00:40:54.840
It was the DeepMind
group at Google.

00:40:54.840 --> 00:40:57.870
And this came out of
some research to try

00:40:57.870 --> 00:41:00.900
and figure out how the
state of the art neural

00:41:00.900 --> 00:41:05.430
networks for image object
recognition and images worked.

00:41:05.430 --> 00:41:10.290
And so, just to simplify
it, basically, playing

00:41:10.290 --> 00:41:11.940
these neural networks
backwards makes

00:41:11.940 --> 00:41:16.350
them hallucinate and create
these beautiful or grotesque,

00:41:16.350 --> 00:41:20.110
depending on your taste, images.

00:41:20.110 --> 00:41:22.560
So this was called Deep
Dream or inception.

00:41:22.560 --> 00:41:27.030
So one of the effects that
this new aesthetic had

00:41:27.030 --> 00:41:31.170
was a lot of artists working
with code or media artists

00:41:31.170 --> 00:41:33.360
started getting interesting
in the potential

00:41:33.360 --> 00:41:36.590
of machine learning and AI.

00:41:36.590 --> 00:41:42.590
And, I was at an event at
Dark Matter in Brooklyn,

00:41:42.590 --> 00:41:48.440
and I saw an artist named Kyle
McDonald, who's presenting

00:41:48.440 --> 00:41:50.000
some of his recent work.

00:41:50.000 --> 00:41:54.380
And he had all these
clusterings of words

00:41:54.380 --> 00:41:59.660
clustered by similarity,
or scales, or images.

00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:03.510
And they all were clustered
in this interesting way.

00:42:03.510 --> 00:42:06.980
And then the last one was
this giant point cloud.

00:42:06.980 --> 00:42:09.140
And as he dragged
his mouse through it,

00:42:09.140 --> 00:42:12.560
I realized every point was a
sample organized by similarity

00:42:12.560 --> 00:42:15.550
and that's it,
that's what I need.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:17.300
So I said, what's this
called, what do you

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:19.250
call that algorithm?

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:22.370
And he said, t-SNE.

00:42:22.370 --> 00:42:26.120
So t-SNE stands for
t-distributed Stochastic

00:42:26.120 --> 00:42:27.380
Neighbor Embedding.

00:42:27.380 --> 00:42:32.360
The t hyphen actually stands
for t hyphen distributed.

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:36.050
So it's a
revolutionary algorithm

00:42:36.050 --> 00:42:39.800
and a revolutionary acronym.

00:42:39.800 --> 00:42:42.860
And, basically,
what this does is

00:42:42.860 --> 00:42:46.530
it's a dimensionality
reduction algorithm.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:52.130
But, in simplest terms, it puts
similar things close together

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:54.540
and doesn't care about
things that are different.

00:42:54.540 --> 00:42:57.570
So these are images, it's
a low resolution image.

00:42:57.570 --> 00:43:02.910
And so, I took some samples
and I ran a t-SNE on it.

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:07.250
So, here, the blue points are
high hats, the red are snare

00:43:07.250 --> 00:43:10.540
and the green are kick drums.

00:43:10.540 --> 00:43:13.050
So this is the
algorithm in action.

00:43:13.050 --> 00:43:15.390
Every little dot
finds its place.

00:43:19.818 --> 00:43:23.262
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:43:32.610 --> 00:43:34.310
So, it worked.

00:43:34.310 --> 00:43:36.560
So, now I had to live code it.

00:43:36.560 --> 00:43:41.600
So, I took 3,000 samples
and I put everything.

00:43:41.600 --> 00:43:45.380
I put marimbas, and
vibraphones, and trumpets,

00:43:45.380 --> 00:43:47.900
as well as all my
percussion samples.

00:43:47.900 --> 00:43:50.150
And I started live coding
traversals through it

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:51.920
and it sounded like This.

00:43:51.920 --> 00:43:55.322
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:07.620
And I memorized this geography.

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:10.020
I knew where a
marimba island was.

00:44:10.020 --> 00:44:12.780
I knew where on the
percussion continent

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:15.690
to find the darbuka
or the djembe.

00:44:19.840 --> 00:44:26.170
But, after working on
it for about two years,

00:44:26.170 --> 00:44:28.810
I figured out there was
a better way to do it.

00:44:28.810 --> 00:44:32.230
And that was, that if I took
out all the tonal samples,

00:44:32.230 --> 00:44:35.590
the marimbas, and the
vibraphones and the trumpets,

00:44:35.590 --> 00:44:39.610
and just had more dry percussion
sounds, what it was actually

00:44:39.610 --> 00:44:44.470
doing was organizing it from
the lowest kick to the highest

00:44:44.470 --> 00:44:46.390
pitched high hat.

00:44:46.390 --> 00:44:51.220
And, it's just that the t-SNE
tries to conserve space,

00:44:51.220 --> 00:44:55.280
so it was crushing it into
some sort of weird curve.

00:44:55.280 --> 00:44:58.740
And so what that meant,
you'll see in a second.

00:45:03.230 --> 00:45:05.660
Basically, you could
draw a curve through it.

00:45:10.760 --> 00:45:13.850
So you draw a curve
through the distribution.

00:45:17.540 --> 00:45:21.500
And I tried all sorts of ways
of curve-fitting algorithms,

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:24.490
but it just always is
better if I did it by hand.

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:27.320
And then you could unwrap it.

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:30.250
And then assign it to a grid.

00:45:30.250 --> 00:45:33.490
And now, there's two
meaningful dimensions.

00:45:33.490 --> 00:45:36.460
So, along this
way, the drums are

00:45:36.460 --> 00:45:38.620
getting from low-pitched
to high-pitched.

00:45:38.620 --> 00:45:41.500
And then on this
axis, it's variations

00:45:41.500 --> 00:45:46.220
within the same
frequency energy.

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:48.620
And so then, I didn't
have to memorize anymore.

00:45:48.620 --> 00:45:56.260
I knew numerically
where the drums were.

00:45:56.260 --> 00:46:00.120
And so, I got commissioned
again by the WaveVR

00:46:00.120 --> 00:46:06.120
to turn the live coding
t-SNE into a VR experience.

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:11.130
And I'll just show you
some early sketches.

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:16.850
So, you're on a giant
laptop and you're watching

00:46:16.850 --> 00:46:18.440
the t-SNE being formed.

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:30.210
You see me code a little bit.

00:46:30.210 --> 00:46:34.830
But then, what's interesting
is, that the users

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:37.200
get to enter, and this
is a three dimensional

00:46:37.200 --> 00:46:41.220
t-SNE of samples, and
make their own rhythms.

00:46:48.192 --> 00:46:51.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:47:27.060 --> 00:47:32.570
So, I started to
think, what the t-SNE

00:47:32.570 --> 00:47:35.360
would do to different kinds
of musical structures.

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:39.590
So this is all the diatonic
seventh chords in every key.

00:47:39.590 --> 00:47:42.050
And I was very
proud of the t-SNE

00:47:42.050 --> 00:47:52.345
because, chords find their
friends, and it makes a circle.

00:47:57.190 --> 00:48:00.460
And so, this is sort
of a circle of fifths.

00:48:00.460 --> 00:48:04.280
And if you unwrap it, again
you get two dimensions.

00:48:04.280 --> 00:48:07.060
One where it's variations
on the same chord.

00:48:07.060 --> 00:48:10.880
And then on the x-axis,
whenever you move over one,

00:48:10.880 --> 00:48:12.340
one note is different.

00:48:12.340 --> 00:48:14.470
If you move over two,
two notes are different.

00:48:14.470 --> 00:48:17.170
And you're always moving
towards the next key

00:48:17.170 --> 00:48:20.400
in the circle of fifths.

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:24.420
So now I can create
progressions without knowing

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:27.270
what chords I'm playing
just by how far I want

00:48:27.270 --> 00:48:29.076
them to change harmonically.

00:48:33.940 --> 00:48:39.930
So, this is a t-SNE of
synth presets interpolating

00:48:39.930 --> 00:48:42.090
between the nearest
[? seven ?] to the mouse.

00:48:42.090 --> 00:48:44.585
[WHOOSHING]

00:48:57.080 --> 00:48:59.840
And the reason that
there's so few points

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:02.870
is, because when you make
a t-SNE, you want to have,

00:49:02.870 --> 00:49:04.760
ideally, a lot of points.

00:49:04.760 --> 00:49:08.990
And, I'm using the
presets to compare,

00:49:08.990 --> 00:49:13.670
is that every commercially
available synth that I've

00:49:13.670 --> 00:49:17.030
been able to find has
modal parameters where

00:49:17.030 --> 00:49:20.720
there's switches, or switching
between waveform or modes.

00:49:20.720 --> 00:49:23.340
And you just can't interpolate
or you get all sorts of clips.

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:27.440
So I curated these because they
all have their modal switches

00:49:27.440 --> 00:49:29.190
at the same place.

00:49:29.190 --> 00:49:33.140
So, if you know a synth
that is fully parametric,

00:49:33.140 --> 00:49:36.960
please come talk to
me after the talk.

00:49:36.960 --> 00:49:41.070
And this is a t-SNE working
on every possible rhythm

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:44.020
with eight beats.

00:49:44.020 --> 00:49:46.240
And, again, the
t-SNE surprised me

00:49:46.240 --> 00:49:49.660
with the topology it
found because it basically

00:49:49.660 --> 00:49:53.520
made this fractal grid.

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:55.740
So that all I had
to do was rotate it.

00:50:00.560 --> 00:50:03.170
And then kind of clean it up.

00:50:03.170 --> 00:50:05.690
And then, again it
has the property

00:50:05.690 --> 00:50:10.920
where if I move over by one, the
beat is different by one beat.

00:50:10.920 --> 00:50:14.810
And if you move over by
two it's different by two.

00:50:14.810 --> 00:50:18.410
I couldn't find any
meaning in the axes,

00:50:18.410 --> 00:50:23.190
but it is an interesting
way to explore rhythms.

00:50:23.190 --> 00:50:25.680
Then, I got a little
distracted from music.

00:50:25.680 --> 00:50:30.590
This is a t-SNE grid of
my former collaborator

00:50:30.590 --> 00:50:33.410
from circus days, B-boy WD-40.

00:50:33.410 --> 00:50:37.250
This is every frame
in a 15 second clip.

00:50:37.250 --> 00:50:39.240
And I don't have time
to show you right now,

00:50:39.240 --> 00:50:41.270
but I've done some
experiments where

00:50:41.270 --> 00:50:46.400
if you move through this
at a fast speed, put, kind

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:47.930
of, triangle waves
through it, you

00:50:47.930 --> 00:50:50.120
can get him to do all
sorts of choreographies

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:53.120
that he never did.

00:50:53.120 --> 00:50:54.860
That's interesting.

00:50:54.860 --> 00:50:58.640
Then, I took this
image of a flower

00:50:58.640 --> 00:51:01.360
and did a t-SNE me
of all the pixels.

00:51:01.360 --> 00:51:05.150
And the background is
just blue to make it pop.

00:51:05.150 --> 00:51:07.840
So this is the t-SNE
unwrapping the pixels.

00:51:16.910 --> 00:51:20.960
And it kills me every
time that it tears here.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:26.480
But, the t-SNE is
optimizing for things

00:51:26.480 --> 00:51:28.910
that are nearby being similar.

00:51:28.910 --> 00:51:31.180
So, sometimes groups
get broken up.

00:51:34.710 --> 00:51:37.020
Then, I think I went
too far, because I

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:42.370
did a t-SNE of the
images in the sequence

00:51:42.370 --> 00:51:45.700
of the t-SNE of the pixels
of the image of the flower.

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:49.720
And by this point, people were
just like messaging me like,

00:51:49.720 --> 00:51:53.740
you OK man, you sure
are tweeting a lot about

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:57.640
[? t-SNE's. ?]
And, truth be told,

00:51:57.640 --> 00:52:00.340
I was literally dreaming
about t-SNE at night.

00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:06.820
So, I started to
think, what else can

00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:12.160
this, kind of, new revival,
and AI, and machine learning

00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:15.820
bring to musical creativity.

00:52:15.820 --> 00:52:22.830
So, when DeepDream
first was released,

00:52:22.830 --> 00:52:25.260
I was really
fascinated by the way

00:52:25.260 --> 00:52:31.230
it could take content and
superimpose new content

00:52:31.230 --> 00:52:35.400
in this, sort of, hallucinatory,
eidetic way, that it

00:52:35.400 --> 00:52:39.000
knows about the shape of
the tree and it fills it in.

00:52:39.000 --> 00:52:43.270
And I talked to some
people working in the field

00:52:43.270 --> 00:52:45.530
and I was like, can
we do this for sound.

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:49.130
I'd love to take the
sound of a babbling brook

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:52.910
and turn it into distant
djembes or something.

00:52:52.910 --> 00:52:55.370
And they're like,
no, this algorithm

00:52:55.370 --> 00:52:58.160
won't work on sound
because this algorithm only

00:52:58.160 --> 00:53:00.410
understands spatial
relationships

00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:04.070
and it can't make sense of time.

00:53:04.070 --> 00:53:06.590
But it only took one more
year until the same group

00:53:06.590 --> 00:53:09.480
at DeepMind created WaveNet.

00:53:09.480 --> 00:53:14.120
And so, the real breakthrough
of WaveNet is speech synthesis.

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:16.610
It's kind of the best
and most natural speech

00:53:16.610 --> 00:53:23.510
synthesis we have yet, and
this just came out in 2016.

00:53:23.510 --> 00:53:26.600
But, of course, I wasn't
interested in speech synthesis.

00:53:26.600 --> 00:53:29.180
I wanted to hear
some weird noises.

00:53:29.180 --> 00:53:33.950
Lucky for me, the lovely
folk at Google Magenta, which

00:53:33.950 --> 00:53:38.690
is a group at Google devoted
to finding how AI and machine

00:53:38.690 --> 00:53:43.020
learning can benefit artists and
creativity, they felt the same.

00:53:43.020 --> 00:53:46.970
So, they trained
WaveNet on 400,000

00:53:46.970 --> 00:53:51.590
individual audio samples of
instruments playing one note.

00:53:51.590 --> 00:53:55.110
And you can see here
this ridiculous encoding.

00:53:55.110 --> 00:54:01.250
This is the original spectrogram
and then, 10 to 15 features,

00:54:01.250 --> 00:54:05.610
and with that they can
reproduce the original waveform.

00:54:05.610 --> 00:54:08.210
So it's happening
on a waveform basis.

00:54:08.210 --> 00:54:13.838
And there's some crunchy
artifacts that turn up

00:54:13.838 --> 00:54:14.880
which, of course, I love.

00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:18.120
I don't know what's going
on with the flugelhorn.

00:54:18.120 --> 00:54:20.880
But what was really cool,
is that you could say,

00:54:20.880 --> 00:54:23.190
I want something
that's 20% base,

00:54:23.190 --> 00:54:26.490
30% glockenspiel,
and 50% flugelhorn.

00:54:26.490 --> 00:54:30.570
And it's not cross-fading
those sounds by amplitude,

00:54:30.570 --> 00:54:34.590
it's generating what it
thinks the mix of those

00:54:34.590 --> 00:54:37.080
would be sample per sample.

00:54:37.080 --> 00:54:43.710
And so, they released a Max for
Live plug-in for Ableton Live.

00:54:43.710 --> 00:54:47.970
And basically, you can
pick different grids

00:54:47.970 --> 00:54:50.070
and you have four
instruments and you

00:54:50.070 --> 00:54:51.900
can interpolate between them.

00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:55.380
In the middle of
these instruments

00:54:55.380 --> 00:55:00.120
you get to these weird places
where it's kind of not in tune

00:55:00.120 --> 00:55:02.940
because the tuning of a
violin for a certain note

00:55:02.940 --> 00:55:06.900
is not the same as a tuning
for, say, a saxophone.

00:55:06.900 --> 00:55:10.980
So you can get these very
strange harmonics and crunchy

00:55:10.980 --> 00:55:12.820
things.

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:18.330
And so, I'm going to perform
that for you tonight.

00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:22.450
So I think this is a fairly
good intro to my work.

00:55:22.450 --> 00:55:25.560
I want to spend the
next hour going deep

00:55:25.560 --> 00:55:28.050
into the philosophical
implications,

00:55:28.050 --> 00:55:30.580
no I'm just kidding.

00:55:30.580 --> 00:55:34.117
I'm going to do a little set up
where I switch the projectors

00:55:34.117 --> 00:55:35.700
and then I'm going
to perform for you.

00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:47.496
