01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:02.540
OK.

01:16:02.540 --> 01:16:04.045
50-50.

01:16:04.045 --> 01:16:05.670
So then that was the
right thing to do.

01:16:05.670 --> 01:16:07.190
I think that's right.

01:16:07.190 --> 01:16:17.162
[INAUDIBLE] But then you get
like the indigo [INAUDIBLE]

01:16:17.162 --> 01:16:18.620
So there's a ton
we can talk about.

01:16:18.620 --> 01:16:20.450
I could spend a
while just asking you

01:16:20.450 --> 01:16:22.770
about details of the code
and what you were doing.

01:16:22.770 --> 01:16:24.228
But I'm going to
resist doing that.

01:16:26.540 --> 01:16:29.350
I also do live coding
of a different sort

01:16:29.350 --> 01:16:31.850
which is, in my class I actually
code in front of the class.

01:16:31.850 --> 01:16:35.060
And that's got its own set
of terrors associated with it

01:16:35.060 --> 01:16:36.830
because it's all MIT students.

01:16:36.830 --> 01:16:39.470
So as soon as you type one wrong
character, they're just like,

01:16:39.470 --> 01:16:41.850
hey you don't need to do that.

01:16:41.850 --> 01:16:44.123
I was pretty low
typo rate today.

01:16:44.123 --> 01:16:45.290
Then at the end I was like--

01:16:48.170 --> 01:16:52.430
I will ask you some broader
questions about that.

01:16:52.430 --> 01:16:55.070
I'm sort of wondering
what's going on in your head

01:16:55.070 --> 01:16:59.630
as you're essentially
constructing this piece.

01:16:59.630 --> 01:17:02.223
And it starts nice and easy.

01:17:02.223 --> 01:17:03.890
There's not a lot of
code on the screen.

01:17:03.890 --> 01:17:07.580
You get the metronome set up and
you get your first basic beats

01:17:07.580 --> 01:17:11.960
going, but then you're
building up more and more.

01:17:11.960 --> 01:17:14.337
And so, I'm wondering what's
going on through your head

01:17:14.337 --> 01:17:15.170
as that's happening?

01:17:15.170 --> 01:17:17.300
And also, does it
ever get overwhelming,

01:17:17.300 --> 01:17:19.660
like do you ever forget,
where did I put that code

01:17:19.660 --> 01:17:21.740
or is it all fairly
clear in your mind

01:17:21.740 --> 01:17:25.640
how it's all architected
and what it's doing?

01:17:25.640 --> 01:17:30.230
Well, because I'm improvising,
sometimes I'll get to a point,

01:17:30.230 --> 01:17:32.780
like today I got
to a point where

01:17:32.780 --> 01:17:41.180
I wanted to stop the drums,
and then immediately take out

01:17:41.180 --> 01:17:44.480
the root note of the chords.

01:17:44.480 --> 01:17:47.750
And I did it and then
I said, Oh, this time

01:17:47.750 --> 01:17:52.880
I put the chords at the top,
and they're not beside this.

01:17:52.880 --> 01:17:54.770
So, all those little
chunks of code

01:17:54.770 --> 01:17:57.710
are called temporal recursions.

01:17:57.710 --> 01:18:00.260
It just schedules
itself to be called

01:18:00.260 --> 01:18:02.910
in the future at a certain time.

01:18:02.910 --> 01:18:05.750
So each one is sort of
a self-contained unit?

01:18:05.750 --> 01:18:06.830
Not everything.

01:18:06.830 --> 01:18:11.030
There's some one-off
commands like, I'm

01:18:11.030 --> 01:18:13.580
sending messages to
this computer on how

01:18:13.580 --> 01:18:16.400
to control the visuals.

01:18:16.400 --> 01:18:19.940
But mostly, Yeah, I set
up a temporal recursion,

01:18:19.940 --> 01:18:23.450
you give it a name, and then
you tell it to call itself back

01:18:23.450 --> 01:18:24.243
in the future.

01:18:24.243 --> 01:18:25.910
And then the ways you
can create rhythms

01:18:25.910 --> 01:18:28.610
are you put
conditionals in there,

01:18:28.610 --> 01:18:30.830
only play on these beats.

01:18:30.830 --> 01:18:33.890
Or, the way I get the
really interesting stuff

01:18:33.890 --> 01:18:39.530
is, I'm actually changing
how soon to call itself back

01:18:39.530 --> 01:18:40.650
in the future.

01:18:40.650 --> 01:18:43.370
So it calls itself,
and then it decides,

01:18:43.370 --> 01:18:46.860
Oh next time I'm going to call
myself an eighth note or maybe

01:18:46.860 --> 01:18:48.140
a triplet.

01:18:48.140 --> 01:18:50.370
And that's where you get
the more interesting sort

01:18:50.370 --> 01:18:50.870
of things.

01:18:50.870 --> 01:18:53.180
So you can get these, sort
of, dynamically forming

01:18:53.180 --> 01:18:54.320
polyrhythms.

01:18:54.320 --> 01:18:56.450
Yeah, and then the
most interesting thing

01:18:56.450 --> 01:18:59.330
which I didn't get
to do, is where

01:18:59.330 --> 01:19:03.620
you can have one call a
different function instead

01:19:03.620 --> 01:19:05.990
and that function
might call it back

01:19:05.990 --> 01:19:07.710
and so they're
feeding off each other

01:19:07.710 --> 01:19:10.940
and passing each
other parameters.

01:19:10.940 --> 01:19:15.110
But I kept it pretty
self-contained.

01:19:15.110 --> 01:19:18.560
I also noticed, you
probably noticed

01:19:18.560 --> 01:19:21.320
but, you're using
random here and there,

01:19:21.320 --> 01:19:26.360
but sparingly, which I
thought was kind of cool.

01:19:26.360 --> 01:19:29.150
You can get quickly carried
away probably with randomness,

01:19:29.150 --> 01:19:30.260
but it's nice to see that.

01:19:30.260 --> 01:19:32.990
Well, so I actually
don't like randomness.

01:19:32.990 --> 01:19:34.340
I try and avoid it.

01:19:34.340 --> 01:19:36.770
But the one place
where I really like

01:19:36.770 --> 01:19:44.450
it is in the parameters of the
oscillators and, specifically,

01:19:44.450 --> 01:19:45.960
the frequencies.

01:19:45.960 --> 01:19:48.470
So I'll say, the
frequency of this

01:19:48.470 --> 01:19:53.300
is, it takes two beats
for this wave to play,

01:19:53.300 --> 01:19:57.510
but one out of three times,
it takes three beats for it

01:19:57.510 --> 01:19:58.430
to play.

01:19:58.430 --> 01:20:01.670
And that that's where I find
the random really interesting.

01:20:04.940 --> 01:20:09.140
And then maybe I'll use it
in the amplitude of the wave,

01:20:09.140 --> 01:20:11.120
and just give it two.

01:20:11.120 --> 01:20:13.310
So, sometimes it's
playing nearby

01:20:13.310 --> 01:20:15.110
and then sometimes
it'll go a bit further.

01:20:15.110 --> 01:20:16.925
But I don't I don't
like just random.

01:20:19.980 --> 01:20:21.500
It's another style.

01:20:21.500 --> 01:20:24.410
Yeah, interesting.

01:20:24.410 --> 01:20:28.280
So I don't I don't do
much improvising, myself,

01:20:28.280 --> 01:20:31.850
as an instrumentalist.

01:20:31.850 --> 01:20:36.620
But I imagine when
you are improvising,

01:20:36.620 --> 01:20:40.080
there's a lot going in your but
it's probably not necessarily

01:20:40.080 --> 01:20:41.610
just the individual note.

01:20:41.610 --> 01:20:45.030
Like, John Coltrane
is not thinking,

01:20:45.030 --> 01:20:48.120
C sharp, D, F natural.

01:20:48.120 --> 01:20:51.330
He's thinking larger
shapes and forms

01:20:51.330 --> 01:20:52.868
as this all was happening.

01:20:52.868 --> 01:20:54.660
And, of course, generally
you're conforming

01:20:54.660 --> 01:20:57.660
to a particular
tune you're playing

01:20:57.660 --> 01:20:59.670
with a certain set
of chord changes.

01:20:59.670 --> 01:21:02.820
But I'm wondering, with this
it all seems very detailed.

01:21:06.840 --> 01:21:10.170
Where do you think you
are, sort of, on this arc?

01:21:10.170 --> 01:21:12.420
Not, not Coltrane.

01:21:12.420 --> 01:21:15.450
Not yet, but, in this
arc where initially,

01:21:15.450 --> 01:21:17.160
this is a new
instrument, essentially,

01:21:17.160 --> 01:21:20.210
and so you start off and
you're just doing scales

01:21:20.210 --> 01:21:21.960
and you're practicing
the individual notes

01:21:21.960 --> 01:21:23.880
and you're getting
accustomed to your instrument

01:21:23.880 --> 01:21:24.963
and learning how it works.

01:21:24.963 --> 01:21:27.270
And then over time, you
are able to start thinking

01:21:27.270 --> 01:21:30.630
in larger scale structures.

01:21:30.630 --> 01:21:34.920
And I wonder if there's an
analog between that learning,

01:21:34.920 --> 01:21:38.310
and development and
cultivating of yourself

01:21:38.310 --> 01:21:42.670
as a musician in the traditional
way versus the system

01:21:42.670 --> 01:21:43.920
that you're working with here.

01:21:43.920 --> 01:21:45.870
Yeah, so there is.

01:21:45.870 --> 01:21:50.850
But the problem is,
I'm also a coder.

01:21:50.850 --> 01:21:55.170
So, I'll practice with a system
and start getting somewhere

01:21:55.170 --> 01:21:58.180
and then I'll think, Oh,
maybe if I just change this.

01:21:58.180 --> 01:22:00.550
And then I'll totally
change the system.

01:22:00.550 --> 01:22:07.470
And I would say almost
100% every show is not

01:22:07.470 --> 01:22:09.330
the same system
as the last show.

01:22:09.330 --> 01:22:10.475
So you can't--

01:22:10.475 --> 01:22:11.850
And wait, sorry,
and when you say

01:22:11.850 --> 01:22:14.225
the system you don't mean like
the particular code you're

01:22:14.225 --> 01:22:17.490
typing now, you mean the
underlying sort of engine,

01:22:17.490 --> 01:22:18.230
so to speak.

01:22:18.230 --> 01:22:22.920
Right, so you can see there's
some functions I call,

01:22:22.920 --> 01:22:24.670
so that's changing.

01:22:24.670 --> 01:22:28.440
And so, extempore, which
is the language I'm using,

01:22:28.440 --> 01:22:30.840
is a bilingual language.

01:22:30.840 --> 01:22:33.630
So I'm writing a scheme here.

01:22:33.630 --> 01:22:36.360
But some of the functions I'm
calling are in what's called,

01:22:36.360 --> 01:22:39.660
[? XT ?] [? Lang. ?] And this
is a low-level language like C,

01:22:39.660 --> 01:22:41.670
but still written like Lisp.

01:22:41.670 --> 01:22:45.210
And so I've built
this whole system for,

01:22:45.210 --> 01:22:50.010
you give it an xy point and
the number of nearest neighbors

01:22:50.010 --> 01:22:54.690
and it'll find those nearest
neighbors in the grid.

01:22:54.690 --> 01:22:57.270
And then based on how
far away they are,

01:22:57.270 --> 01:23:02.490
kind of share the
amplitude and the panning.

01:23:02.490 --> 01:23:04.770
And all of that is written
in this lower level.

01:23:04.770 --> 01:23:07.140
And I'm constantly
changing that too,

01:23:07.140 --> 01:23:12.000
so the semantics are changing,
the function is changing.

01:23:12.000 --> 01:23:16.290
And I keep saying I want to stop
and then just practice with it,

01:23:16.290 --> 01:23:19.200
but it never happens,
so there's that.

01:23:19.200 --> 01:23:23.790
But, also to your
point about Coltrane's

01:23:23.790 --> 01:23:27.030
not thinking of what
the specific note is.

01:23:27.030 --> 01:23:28.560
I don't know if it
was him who said,

01:23:28.560 --> 01:23:32.910
they asked him his advice for
young musicians and he said,

01:23:32.910 --> 01:23:38.980
learn your scales and then
forget all that bullshit.

01:23:38.980 --> 01:23:42.780
And so, like I said there's
the declarative, like,

01:23:42.780 --> 01:23:46.890
play this note now,
play this drum now.

01:23:46.890 --> 01:23:51.060
And, I'm at least
one step above that

01:23:51.060 --> 01:23:55.260
where I'm shaping waves
using amplitude and frequency

01:23:55.260 --> 01:23:57.840
modulation to create
these complex waves

01:23:57.840 --> 01:24:00.030
and then sampling from
them, rhythmically,

01:24:00.030 --> 01:24:02.160
to pick notes from scales.

01:24:02.160 --> 01:24:05.790
And that really seems like
this pretty nice ingenuity.

01:24:05.790 --> 01:24:08.087
And I think what we saw
here initially is the grid.

01:24:08.087 --> 01:24:09.420
That's your unrolled t-SNE grid.

01:24:09.420 --> 01:24:11.545
That's the unrolled t-SNE
and then it rolls back up

01:24:11.545 --> 01:24:13.410
and then it goes to
the three dimensions.

01:24:13.410 --> 01:24:15.960
Right, but in terms
of how you organize

01:24:15.960 --> 01:24:19.830
your samples, that's essentially
a thing that gets you--

01:24:19.830 --> 01:24:22.360
I'm always giving
numbers to the grid,

01:24:22.360 --> 01:24:25.703
regardless of what it's showing.

01:24:25.703 --> 01:24:27.870
But in terms of just, you're
saying you don't really

01:24:27.870 --> 01:24:29.910
choose a note anymore.

01:24:29.910 --> 01:24:32.550
You are thinking
one level higher

01:24:32.550 --> 01:24:34.530
now because you
essentially have this sort

01:24:34.530 --> 01:24:36.870
of spatial
organization of sound.

01:24:36.870 --> 01:24:38.910
And now you're thinking
of traversing through

01:24:38.910 --> 01:24:41.130
that as opposed
to saying, I want

01:24:41.130 --> 01:24:43.380
this one particular high hat.

01:24:43.380 --> 01:24:45.000
Yeah and there's
even more than that

01:24:45.000 --> 01:24:48.120
because I can't even say I
want this particular high hat,

01:24:48.120 --> 01:24:50.610
because there was one
drum that I really wanted

01:24:50.610 --> 01:24:53.880
to play because it
goes like [BEATBOXING]

01:24:53.880 --> 01:24:55.950
and is really cool.

01:24:55.950 --> 01:24:59.760
And it's at position x 14y zero.

01:24:59.760 --> 01:25:01.660
But, every time I play--

01:25:01.660 --> 01:25:03.450
That's the best position for it.

01:25:03.450 --> 01:25:06.300
Well, it's memorable.

01:25:06.300 --> 01:25:08.100
I will always remember that.

01:25:08.100 --> 01:25:09.640
Remember it forever.

01:25:09.640 --> 01:25:12.780
But, what else I'm doing is,
so I'm creating these chord

01:25:12.780 --> 01:25:15.240
progressions by
putting the sine waves

01:25:15.240 --> 01:25:17.220
through that grid of chords.

01:25:17.220 --> 01:25:19.440
But, when every one
of those drum samples

01:25:19.440 --> 01:25:23.370
is tagged with the approximate
pitch, and when I play a chord,

01:25:23.370 --> 01:25:26.280
only drums that are tagged
with one of those four pitches

01:25:26.280 --> 01:25:29.100
are going to be chosen from
the algorithm that finds

01:25:29.100 --> 01:25:31.440
the end nearest neighbors.

01:25:31.440 --> 01:25:34.230
So, if I put in [? 14-zero ?]
like I did at the end,

01:25:34.230 --> 01:25:35.243
I was like, Oh, come on.

01:25:35.243 --> 01:25:37.410
It's got to hit the chord
where it plays that sound,

01:25:37.410 --> 01:25:38.880
but it didn't.

01:25:38.880 --> 01:25:42.030
So I can't choose
the exact sound.

01:25:42.030 --> 01:25:44.340
It's almost like a
limitation of the system

01:25:44.340 --> 01:25:46.560
that you've built
yourself in order

01:25:46.560 --> 01:25:48.220
to be able to achieve
a higher level--

01:25:48.220 --> 01:25:51.210
So when I play with Mae,
with Scorpion Mouse,

01:25:51.210 --> 01:25:53.310
sometimes we're
jamming and she's like,

01:25:53.310 --> 01:25:56.190
can you change that
third chord, like I

01:25:56.190 --> 01:25:58.640
don't like the way the
dominant goes to the three.

01:25:58.640 --> 01:26:03.630
And I'm like, can you tell me
when the third chor-- like,

01:26:03.630 --> 01:26:05.220
I'm like, no.

01:26:05.220 --> 01:26:07.710
So I don't have that, I'm
too high level that when

01:26:07.710 --> 01:26:14.760
I work with human musicians,
there's definitely a language

01:26:14.760 --> 01:26:17.730
barrier where I'm trying to
figure out, what do they expect

01:26:17.730 --> 01:26:19.530
or how could I do that.

01:26:19.530 --> 01:26:21.450
But it's also a
really good challenge

01:26:21.450 --> 01:26:26.610
that helps me grow is trying
to work with musicians.

01:26:26.610 --> 01:26:28.650
As opposed to, say, you, right?

01:26:28.650 --> 01:26:32.190
Me or like, so I've been
to a bunch of live coding

01:26:32.190 --> 01:26:35.640
conferences and they'll have
on-site live coding ensembles.

01:26:35.640 --> 01:26:37.200
It's like, holy shi--

01:26:37.200 --> 01:26:39.390
wow.

01:26:39.390 --> 01:26:42.090
I don't even think they
know what sound they're

01:26:42.090 --> 01:26:46.800
making, because everyone
can make every sound--

01:26:46.800 --> 01:26:50.730
Oh so it just all blends
together into this wild--

01:26:50.730 --> 01:26:54.900
I mean, everyone has
access to oscillators,

01:26:54.900 --> 01:26:57.780
and samples, and
noise and filters

01:26:57.780 --> 01:26:59.400
and they're all typing away.

01:27:03.630 --> 01:27:04.560
Any questions?

01:27:04.560 --> 01:27:06.643
I thought I'd open up to
questions of the audience

01:27:06.643 --> 01:27:09.760
and, let's see, there's a
microphone going around.

01:27:09.760 --> 01:27:10.470
Oh cool, OK.

01:27:10.470 --> 01:27:13.597
So, speak into the mic so we
can get the question recorded.

01:27:13.597 --> 01:27:14.430
But, yeah, go ahead.

01:27:14.430 --> 01:27:16.530
Here.

01:27:16.530 --> 01:27:19.290
What happens when you get
an error on your code?

01:27:19.290 --> 01:27:22.380
Does it just not play that part?

01:27:22.380 --> 01:27:26.100
Yeah I did miss a parameter.

01:27:29.670 --> 01:27:31.890
So, because it's a
dynamic language, when

01:27:31.890 --> 01:27:33.990
it goes to call
itself in the future

01:27:33.990 --> 01:27:35.700
and the new version
has an error,

01:27:35.700 --> 01:27:38.117
it just doesn't play it and I
get an error in the console.

01:27:41.430 --> 01:27:43.420
There's another version.

01:27:43.420 --> 01:27:46.050
So that's, I'm using the
dynamics scheme language.

01:27:46.050 --> 01:27:49.320
You can also do all this
with the low-level language.

01:27:49.320 --> 01:27:51.780
And if you make
an error there, it

01:27:51.780 --> 01:27:54.060
doesn't replace the function.

01:27:54.060 --> 01:27:56.890
If it doesn't compile,
it won't do it.

01:27:56.890 --> 01:27:58.540
So in a way that's better.

01:27:58.540 --> 01:28:01.200
But then you have to
program with types.

01:28:01.200 --> 01:28:03.900
And I just can't,
I just can't do it.

01:28:03.900 --> 01:28:05.290
It slows me down.

01:28:05.290 --> 01:28:05.790
Yeah.

01:28:05.790 --> 01:28:07.248
And with this being
interpreted, it

01:28:07.248 --> 01:28:08.850
doesn't know that
there is a bug in it

01:28:08.850 --> 01:28:09.810
until it actually
tries to execute it.

01:28:09.810 --> 01:28:12.030
Until it executes it
and then it stops.

01:28:12.030 --> 01:28:13.590
Yeah.

01:28:13.590 --> 01:28:17.010
OK, yes, over here.

01:28:17.010 --> 01:28:18.810
Yes, just wait for the mic.

01:28:18.810 --> 01:28:20.070
Thank you.

01:28:20.070 --> 01:28:23.550
So in an ensemble
situation, some

01:28:23.550 --> 01:28:25.650
of the music that
you're performing today

01:28:25.650 --> 01:28:29.050
so rhythmically is kind
of the basis for it.

01:28:29.050 --> 01:28:33.450
So, do you synchronize
clock with other people

01:28:33.450 --> 01:28:38.143
or and I'll ask separately if
you're playing with a drummer,

01:28:38.143 --> 01:28:39.310
I don't know if you do that.

01:28:39.310 --> 01:28:42.630
But if you're playing with
a live percussionist, what's

01:28:42.630 --> 01:28:46.010
your concept on the
rhythmic framework?

01:28:46.010 --> 01:28:48.960
I try to avoid playing
with live drummers.

01:28:48.960 --> 01:28:52.080
It's happened but,
it's never going

01:28:52.080 --> 01:28:58.680
to work because, I
can't groove with them.

01:28:58.680 --> 01:29:01.590
If you tell me
exactly, like, you

01:29:01.590 --> 01:29:07.290
want the snare a 30 second note
late, I can do that easily.

01:29:07.290 --> 01:29:11.250
But, natural drummers
have a groove,

01:29:11.250 --> 01:29:12.960
the way they speed
up and slow down

01:29:12.960 --> 01:29:15.210
and they move with
the whole band.

01:29:15.210 --> 01:29:19.080
And so, basically, it's like
they have to drum to a click,

01:29:19.080 --> 01:29:20.340
except it's not a click.

01:29:20.340 --> 01:29:23.100
It's my really strange rhythms.

01:29:23.100 --> 01:29:26.235
And I just think, frequency-wise
there's too much overlap.

01:29:29.510 --> 01:29:33.020
So then for when I work
with other live coders,

01:29:33.020 --> 01:29:36.080
yes, synchronize clock.

01:29:36.080 --> 01:29:38.390
Synchronized clock
sometimes is a problem

01:29:38.390 --> 01:29:41.510
because there's a lot of
really popular live coding

01:29:41.510 --> 01:29:46.790
languages like, Tidal and
SuperCollider and ChucK

01:29:46.790 --> 01:29:50.000
and, that's what I'm
missing, Sonic Pi.

01:29:50.000 --> 01:29:55.160
And these ones often will
work with each other.

01:29:55.160 --> 01:29:58.190
But, I'm kind of
like lone wolf here.

01:29:58.190 --> 01:30:00.890
Extempore, I mean there's some
other uses around the world

01:30:00.890 --> 01:30:04.400
but, mainly, it's the
creator and his collaborators

01:30:04.400 --> 01:30:06.440
in Australia.

01:30:06.440 --> 01:30:10.140
So, sometimes it is a bit tough.

01:30:10.140 --> 01:30:12.690
And I'll just send
OSC messages saying,

01:30:12.690 --> 01:30:15.860
hey keep up to this
time, you should be here.

01:30:19.510 --> 01:30:21.693
How about over here.

01:30:21.693 --> 01:30:23.110
Just kind of working
our way back.

01:30:26.910 --> 01:30:29.640
The VR t-SNE experience
that you showed,

01:30:29.640 --> 01:30:33.690
was that being
live coded as well?

01:30:33.690 --> 01:30:35.280
Or is it something,
like could you--

01:30:35.280 --> 01:30:37.020
Kind of.

01:30:37.020 --> 01:30:41.470
So, I actually, I don't
know what I was thinking.

01:30:41.470 --> 01:30:42.900
I'm bad at faking things.

01:30:42.900 --> 01:30:46.110
I always want it to actually
be generative and interactive.

01:30:46.110 --> 01:30:49.170
So, I built a system
in Unity whereby,

01:30:49.170 --> 01:30:51.900
whenever I was live
coding, I was live

01:30:51.900 --> 01:30:56.640
coding the system in Unity,
and I was procedurally

01:30:56.640 --> 01:31:03.180
creating animation events so
that it captured my performance

01:31:03.180 --> 01:31:05.040
and we would actually
[? replay ?] it.

01:31:05.040 --> 01:31:07.920
And it would also send
the code that I had typed

01:31:07.920 --> 01:31:09.510
and then type it in unity.

01:31:12.090 --> 01:31:13.920
And, no one, people
who were just

01:31:13.920 --> 01:31:17.460
like, that's a video, right.

01:31:17.460 --> 01:31:18.720
Our friend, Adam.

01:31:18.720 --> 01:31:21.630
Oh Yeah, Yeah.

01:31:21.630 --> 01:31:23.490
But it was real.

01:31:23.490 --> 01:31:24.580
Real.

01:31:24.580 --> 01:31:26.920
Whatever that means.

01:31:26.920 --> 01:31:27.420
Let's see.

01:31:27.420 --> 01:31:28.212
Let's go over here.

01:31:30.830 --> 01:31:32.430
Thanks a lot.

01:31:32.430 --> 01:31:35.270
I'm really interested in new
kinds of live performance

01:31:35.270 --> 01:31:40.850
and I'm wondering what you think
the vibe of algorave can do.

01:31:40.850 --> 01:31:43.040
Or what's a new
kind of experience

01:31:43.040 --> 01:31:47.120
that could come out of this
kind of live music performance?

01:31:47.120 --> 01:31:53.660
Well, traditionally, like back
in 2012, the way people danced,

01:31:53.660 --> 01:31:55.580
there was like an
algorave dance.

01:31:55.580 --> 01:31:57.800
And it was just
a bunch of geeks.

01:31:57.800 --> 01:31:59.930
You had beer in
one hand and you're

01:31:59.930 --> 01:32:03.780
reading the code and
kind of going like this.

01:32:03.780 --> 01:32:06.440
And so that's pretty boring.

01:32:06.440 --> 01:32:08.180
But now, we've been
doing these shows

01:32:08.180 --> 01:32:11.930
in New York and all kind
of lifting our chops

01:32:11.930 --> 01:32:14.960
levels up and posting on
all sorts of weird lists.

01:32:14.960 --> 01:32:20.150
And now would get all these
tech-savvy raver kids.

01:32:20.150 --> 01:32:23.540
We don't know where they came
from or how they found us.

01:32:23.540 --> 01:32:25.820
But they just dance.

01:32:25.820 --> 01:32:31.520
They love this weird,
kind of, alien music,

01:32:31.520 --> 01:32:33.320
and the vibe is really good.

01:32:33.320 --> 01:32:38.340
And tomorrow I'm
missing our algorave

01:32:38.340 --> 01:32:41.390
at [? Ibeam ?] in New York.

01:32:41.390 --> 01:32:45.500
And, on April 20th, we're going
to play our biggest show ever

01:32:45.500 --> 01:32:50.540
at this legendary, it's called
Performance Space, in New York,

01:32:50.540 --> 01:32:53.720
and it's been kind
of a breeding ground

01:32:53.720 --> 01:32:57.830
for experimental performance
and dance since the 80s.

01:32:57.830 --> 01:32:59.810
And they just made
this new venue.

01:32:59.810 --> 01:33:02.390
And they reached out to
us and they were like,

01:33:02.390 --> 01:33:03.720
we want to try an algorave.

01:33:03.720 --> 01:33:05.990
And we were like, do
you know what it is?

01:33:05.990 --> 01:33:07.460
Are you sure?

01:33:07.460 --> 01:33:11.330
But, they have
two 14k projectors

01:33:11.330 --> 01:33:13.610
and seven 7K projectors.

01:33:13.610 --> 01:33:18.620
So you are going to be
blinded by code at this show.

01:33:18.620 --> 01:33:22.370
And it's in collaboration with
a group called Babycastles

01:33:22.370 --> 01:33:24.720
that do indie video games.

01:33:24.720 --> 01:33:27.020
So there's going to be
arcades everywhere, there's

01:33:27.020 --> 01:33:31.070
going to be people wearing
arcades coming on the dance

01:33:31.070 --> 01:33:33.380
floor and you could actually
play the arcade on them.

01:33:33.380 --> 01:33:38.190
So, I feel like this feels like
a kind of futuristic that like,

01:33:38.190 --> 01:33:42.380
hey, there's a lot of
geeks now and sometimes

01:33:42.380 --> 01:33:44.840
they like to dance.

01:33:44.840 --> 01:33:45.980
We like to dance, it's OK.

01:33:48.570 --> 01:33:49.070
Who else?

01:33:49.070 --> 01:33:51.055
There were some back there.

01:33:51.055 --> 01:33:52.430
Let's go all the
way in the back.

01:33:58.890 --> 01:34:01.950
I was wondering what your
relationship with the audience

01:34:01.950 --> 01:34:04.890
is when you're performing
because on the one hand,

01:34:04.890 --> 01:34:09.187
live performance is very like,
you're aware of your audience

01:34:09.187 --> 01:34:10.270
that you're being watched.

01:34:10.270 --> 01:34:12.660
But on the other
hand, when I'm coding,

01:34:12.660 --> 01:34:15.370
I tend to just zone
out what's around me.

01:34:15.370 --> 01:34:19.140
So I was wondering where you
sort of are on that spectrum.

01:34:19.140 --> 01:34:24.030
Yeah, for me this is the
tragedy of live coding.

01:34:24.030 --> 01:34:26.820
I come from a background of
being a very live and physical

01:34:26.820 --> 01:34:30.960
performer and just being
keenly aware of the audience,

01:34:30.960 --> 01:34:34.350
more than knowing they're
watching me just really feeling

01:34:34.350 --> 01:34:35.280
the vibe.

01:34:35.280 --> 01:34:39.930
And, yeah, you can't do
it when you're coding.

01:34:39.930 --> 01:34:43.590
You can barely hear the music
when you're coding, really.

01:34:43.590 --> 01:34:45.600
I mean, it's still
going in your ears,

01:34:45.600 --> 01:34:48.150
but you're just so
razor focused on,

01:34:48.150 --> 01:34:52.200
how do I get the next thing to
happen that, you're definitely

01:34:52.200 --> 01:34:53.460
not embodied.

01:34:53.460 --> 01:34:54.990
If anything, it's the opposite.

01:34:54.990 --> 01:34:59.760
And, like I said, it doesn't
matter how you type the keys.

01:34:59.760 --> 01:35:02.820
No one will feel it.

01:35:02.820 --> 01:35:04.860
Sometimes I hit the
eval button extra hard.

01:35:04.860 --> 01:35:09.020
That's the one action moment
in live coding is evaluate.

01:35:12.080 --> 01:35:18.870
But Yeah, so, what my dream
was for this performance but,

01:35:18.870 --> 01:35:23.400
I can't find anyone who
will leak one to me,

01:35:23.400 --> 01:35:27.810
I want to get a Magic Leap
so that there's no laptop

01:35:27.810 --> 01:35:30.810
and that I'm looking
at the audience, maybe,

01:35:30.810 --> 01:35:33.510
and the code is floating
somewhere out there.

01:35:33.510 --> 01:35:35.340
So that's my next step.

01:35:35.340 --> 01:35:40.050
I tried it with the HoloLens
and I had such a headache

01:35:40.050 --> 01:35:41.820
that I couldn't
code for two days.

01:35:41.820 --> 01:35:43.660
So that was off the table.

01:35:43.660 --> 01:35:46.920
It might still be a while before
you get what you really need.

01:35:46.920 --> 01:35:50.710
But I think that, to me, seems
like it could be the solution.

01:35:50.710 --> 01:35:52.260
That'd be one solution.

01:35:52.260 --> 01:35:57.180
And the second is, I keep
getting to this point

01:35:57.180 --> 01:36:00.360
where I'm live coding, like
sometimes I'll live code

01:36:00.360 --> 01:36:02.730
and it'll be really algorithmic.

01:36:02.730 --> 01:36:08.640
Like all sorts of loops and
iteration and nested 'if'

01:36:08.640 --> 01:36:11.740
statements and these
really emergent properties.

01:36:11.740 --> 01:36:14.040
And then as I find
what's cool in that,

01:36:14.040 --> 01:36:17.700
I start abstracting it into
these little functions.

01:36:17.700 --> 01:36:19.620
And I start just using
these little functions

01:36:19.620 --> 01:36:22.650
like instruments and then I
say, hey, how many functions

01:36:22.650 --> 01:36:24.240
am I really using, 10?

01:36:24.240 --> 01:36:25.980
And so it makes me
wonder if there's

01:36:25.980 --> 01:36:30.690
a way to build a system in
virtual reality, for instance.

01:36:30.690 --> 01:36:33.570
Or mixed reality,
where you're coding,

01:36:33.570 --> 01:36:35.265
but you're physically
moving around.

01:36:38.773 --> 01:36:41.190
Like if there was a notion of
taking little chunks of code

01:36:41.190 --> 01:36:45.120
and physically locating
them which might also just

01:36:45.120 --> 01:36:47.765
help you spatially think
of the music in the space

01:36:47.765 --> 01:36:49.140
and structurally
what's going on.

01:36:49.140 --> 01:36:53.050
Yeah and then if it was,
again, Magic Leap, then I

01:36:53.050 --> 01:36:55.620
feel like, it's like, I'm
here, the code is here

01:36:55.620 --> 01:36:57.210
and I'm putting it around.

01:36:57.210 --> 01:36:59.190
That would be cool.

01:36:59.190 --> 01:37:00.535
Give me five years.

01:37:00.535 --> 01:37:02.160
OK and then, there's
one question right

01:37:02.160 --> 01:37:05.130
at the very back.

01:37:05.130 --> 01:37:08.910
Hi so, I'll comment first,
your t-SNE in a t-SNE

01:37:08.910 --> 01:37:13.570
reminded me of Escher,
so thanks for that.

01:37:13.570 --> 01:37:15.870
So, I just started
engineering school

01:37:15.870 --> 01:37:18.270
but I'm also a massage
therapist and a musician.

01:37:18.270 --> 01:37:20.460
So something I
think about often,

01:37:20.460 --> 01:37:24.930
is when I'm moving my clients
through body movements,

01:37:24.930 --> 01:37:28.200
I'm usually thinking, I
wish I could hear or see

01:37:28.200 --> 01:37:29.940
their stickiness.

01:37:29.940 --> 01:37:34.375
And so, because of the Kinect
you were talking about,

01:37:34.375 --> 01:37:36.000
I'm wondering, is
there anything you're

01:37:36.000 --> 01:37:38.970
thinking about in health
care applications at all?

01:37:38.970 --> 01:37:43.500
Yeah, I've always wanted
to build a Kinect app that

01:37:43.500 --> 01:37:46.230
is like, hey, you're slouching.

01:37:46.230 --> 01:37:52.230
You slouched for 95%
of today, or something.

01:37:52.230 --> 01:37:55.800
And I never got around to it.

01:37:55.800 --> 01:37:59.010
I don't know if Kinect
is the best way.

01:37:59.010 --> 01:38:03.180
It's not very precise
to that level.

01:38:03.180 --> 01:38:07.500
Like, I think these little
tightnesses are really precise.

01:38:07.500 --> 01:38:13.440
But I did see a new
technology recently.

01:38:13.440 --> 01:38:14.880
I forget what it's
called but, it

01:38:14.880 --> 01:38:18.450
looks like one of those
Apple mouse things

01:38:18.450 --> 01:38:21.570
and it sticks on
your back right here.

01:38:21.570 --> 01:38:23.070
Oh, you guys know what it is.

01:38:23.070 --> 01:38:25.470
And it measures the
angle of your spine

01:38:25.470 --> 01:38:29.770
and it'll buzz you if
you slouch too much.

01:38:29.770 --> 01:38:30.780
And so I want to get it.

01:38:30.780 --> 01:38:34.590
I don't know if I'll
be able to stand it.

01:38:34.590 --> 01:38:37.320
But I think there's a lot,
like I recently started

01:38:37.320 --> 01:38:41.790
using the Muse headband.

01:38:41.790 --> 01:38:46.840
I've been super skeptical
of EEG's but, this one

01:38:46.840 --> 01:38:52.110
is pretty impressive for
the one little task it does.

01:38:52.110 --> 01:38:54.060
And I sort of was
thinking about using,

01:38:54.060 --> 01:38:58.880
the Muse is that EEG headset,
kind of consumer-grade, very

01:38:58.880 --> 01:39:00.710
low spatial resolution.

01:39:00.710 --> 01:39:04.940
But, supposedly will
tell you if you're, no,

01:39:04.940 --> 01:39:06.530
I wouldn't say supposedly.

01:39:06.530 --> 01:39:09.200
It definitely tells you
when your mind is active

01:39:09.200 --> 01:39:13.593
and when you're
chill and focused.

01:39:13.593 --> 01:39:15.260
And I've even tried
live coding with it.

01:39:15.260 --> 01:39:17.370
So I thought,
maybe, if I had that

01:39:17.370 --> 01:39:19.550
and that was an
input into the chain,

01:39:19.550 --> 01:39:23.870
there could be a little bit
of the user's experience.

01:39:23.870 --> 01:39:27.500
I don't know exactly how
that answers your question

01:39:27.500 --> 01:39:29.480
but, I'm interested.

01:39:29.480 --> 01:39:37.120
As a performer, I think the
current state of computers

01:39:37.120 --> 01:39:40.990
is like, the less you use
them the healthier you are.

01:39:40.990 --> 01:39:43.360
But, unfortunately,
in 2018 there

01:39:43.360 --> 01:39:47.050
is nothing you can do that's
useful without a computer.

01:39:47.050 --> 01:39:49.810
So, I feel like it's the
computer that has to change.

01:39:49.810 --> 01:39:53.950
And you can all come up and
see my keyboard later on.

01:39:53.950 --> 01:39:54.775
Very ergo keyboard.

01:39:57.460 --> 01:39:59.290
Cool.

01:39:59.290 --> 01:40:02.050
Let's see, back over there.

01:40:02.050 --> 01:40:02.620
Yes, you.

01:40:06.090 --> 01:40:07.800
So, I have two questions.

01:40:07.800 --> 01:40:10.290
One is, is there
any situation where

01:40:10.290 --> 01:40:13.560
you change what you
described as the system,

01:40:13.560 --> 01:40:16.450
or the underlying
functions live?

01:40:16.450 --> 01:40:19.710
And then my second question
is, how do you teach this

01:40:19.710 --> 01:40:23.970
or how would someone
go about learning this?

01:40:23.970 --> 01:40:28.740
Does it require extensive
experience improvising

01:40:28.740 --> 01:40:34.680
in other music or
in music in general?

01:40:34.680 --> 01:40:36.670
Wait, say the first question.

01:40:36.670 --> 01:40:39.960
What was the first question?

01:40:39.960 --> 01:40:42.085
The first question was, so
you talked about how you

01:40:42.085 --> 01:40:43.293
changed the underlying system

01:40:43.293 --> 01:40:43.887
I remember.

01:40:48.660 --> 01:40:51.210
I try to avoid that live.

01:40:51.210 --> 01:40:53.850
I do it all the time at home.

01:40:53.850 --> 01:40:56.670
But it's definitely
risky because if you

01:40:56.670 --> 01:40:58.380
make a mistake at
the low level, I

01:40:58.380 --> 01:41:00.030
don't mean like
a doesn't compile

01:41:00.030 --> 01:41:05.370
mistake, I mean it's not memory
managed, a low-level one.

01:41:05.370 --> 01:41:08.610
So you could create a
memory leak in real time

01:41:08.610 --> 01:41:10.680
while you're coding.

01:41:10.680 --> 01:41:14.820
And I've also just done
something recursive

01:41:14.820 --> 01:41:16.320
where I call a
function and I didn't

01:41:16.320 --> 01:41:19.740
realize that, that function
calls a function that sometimes

01:41:19.740 --> 01:41:24.120
calls that function, and
just blow up the stack.

01:41:24.120 --> 01:41:26.100
But I would like to do that.

01:41:26.100 --> 01:41:28.470
I think that's like
as cool as it can get,

01:41:28.470 --> 01:41:31.290
to change that low-level stuff.

01:41:31.290 --> 01:41:35.670
And I did use Unity
for the visuals

01:41:35.670 --> 01:41:39.900
today because I'm always
toying with making this

01:41:39.900 --> 01:41:41.190
into a VR experience.

01:41:41.190 --> 01:41:48.090
But Extempore actually
has NanoVG and OpenGL,

01:41:48.090 --> 01:41:51.610
and even [? an ?] open
source video game engine.

01:41:51.610 --> 01:41:53.110
So you can do all
the visuals in it.

01:41:53.110 --> 01:41:56.280
So, I think the most
interesting thing

01:41:56.280 --> 01:41:58.470
is where you build
a system, then

01:41:58.470 --> 01:42:01.680
use the high-level language
to put some patterns into it

01:42:01.680 --> 01:42:05.070
and then you change the system
with those patterns in it.

01:42:05.070 --> 01:42:06.730
And then the second thing was--

01:42:06.730 --> 01:42:08.730
The second thing is, how
do you teach the stuff?

01:42:08.730 --> 01:42:10.050
How do you teach your stuff.

01:42:10.050 --> 01:42:11.910
Oh, it's great fun.

01:42:11.910 --> 01:42:13.830
If you're learning
on your own, I

01:42:13.830 --> 01:42:16.800
would not recommend Extempore.

01:42:16.800 --> 01:42:21.420
I mostly wouldn't recommend
Extempore to anyone.

01:42:21.420 --> 01:42:23.490
No, well I mean because, so--

01:42:23.490 --> 01:42:25.170
Like I would never
join a club that

01:42:25.170 --> 01:42:26.670
would accept me as a member.

01:42:26.670 --> 01:42:35.520
No, no, it's more like, what's
his name, Fear and Loathing.

01:42:35.520 --> 01:42:37.710
Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas.

01:42:37.710 --> 01:42:39.060
Hunter S. Thompson.

01:42:39.060 --> 01:42:40.620
At the beginning of
his book he says,

01:42:40.620 --> 01:42:42.760
I would never recommend
drugs to anyone,

01:42:42.760 --> 01:42:45.120
they just worked for me.

01:42:45.120 --> 01:42:49.050
And, Yeah, so the creator of
Extempore, he specifically

01:42:49.050 --> 01:42:55.740
said, there's this trend of
easy-to-use live coding tools

01:42:55.740 --> 01:42:59.250
and this is just saying no.

01:42:59.250 --> 01:43:02.070
This is going to give
you all the possibility

01:43:02.070 --> 01:43:06.310
and all the power there is, and
it's really, really incredible.

01:43:06.310 --> 01:43:09.772
I mean he controls robotic
telescopes with it.

01:43:09.772 --> 01:43:11.730
So you're saying maybe
this is the second thing

01:43:11.730 --> 01:43:12.630
you should learn.

01:43:12.630 --> 01:43:15.090
Yeah, well, I mean look, if
you have a strong engineering

01:43:15.090 --> 01:43:18.460
background, you'll
figure it out easy.

01:43:18.460 --> 01:43:21.630
But if you're talking
about jump into it easily,

01:43:21.630 --> 01:43:23.160
there's these other languages.

01:43:23.160 --> 01:43:28.170
Especially Sonic Pi,
which is Ruby, and just

01:43:28.170 --> 01:43:33.030
made with musicians in
mind, and it's very easy.

01:43:33.030 --> 01:43:36.930
It doesn't matter if you have
a background in improvisation.

01:43:36.930 --> 01:43:39.120
And then there's
other ones like Tidal,

01:43:39.120 --> 01:43:41.850
which is this Haskell
language that's

01:43:41.850 --> 01:43:44.940
all combining these
little operators to create

01:43:44.940 --> 01:43:46.230
these emergent rhythms.

01:43:46.230 --> 01:43:49.050
And that, you could just
mess with the numbers

01:43:49.050 --> 01:43:53.598
and the order of the functions
and just see what you like.

01:43:53.598 --> 01:43:55.140
So, there's languages
out there where

01:43:55.140 --> 01:43:58.470
you could download it tonight
and start making cool beats.

01:43:58.470 --> 01:44:01.043
But, Extempore is
more like a full,

01:44:01.043 --> 01:44:03.210
it's not a [? library ?]
[? type ?] [? of ?] [? a ?]

01:44:03.210 --> 01:44:06.098
language, it's its own language.

01:44:06.098 --> 01:44:07.890
And then, the other
answer to that question

01:44:07.890 --> 01:44:09.810
is, on Thursday night,
Jason is actually

01:44:09.810 --> 01:44:13.730
teaching a class about this.

01:44:13.730 --> 01:44:15.480
So folks who are
attending will, I think,

01:44:15.480 --> 01:44:17.550
will leave the
class, essentially

01:44:17.550 --> 01:44:19.350
having a basic feel
for how it works

01:44:19.350 --> 01:44:21.433
and being able to make
their own live coded beats.

01:44:21.433 --> 01:44:24.210
Yeah, and usually what
I find when I teach,

01:44:24.210 --> 01:44:27.630
I teach the very basics
like, specify what note when,

01:44:27.630 --> 01:44:28.650
kind of thing.

01:44:28.650 --> 01:44:31.830
And, everyone takes it
in their own direction.

01:44:31.830 --> 01:44:33.780
And half of the things
people do, I'm like,

01:44:33.780 --> 01:44:36.750
I've never thought
of doing that.

01:44:36.750 --> 01:44:40.500
Which actually, it kind of
reminds me of something,

01:44:40.500 --> 01:44:44.160
I think you said, when we were
working on Harmonics, which

01:44:44.160 --> 01:44:49.590
was, like one of the best
parts of this very rapid coding

01:44:49.590 --> 01:44:53.200
is actually the accidents and
the mistakes and the bugs.

01:44:53.200 --> 01:44:56.670
And when a bug turns
into a feature, or a bug

01:44:56.670 --> 01:44:59.850
turns into sort of a new,
interesting artistic concept

01:44:59.850 --> 01:45:01.750
that you wouldn't have
thought of otherwise.

01:45:01.750 --> 01:45:04.620
And then you save it.

01:45:04.620 --> 01:45:06.840
All my errors are
better than my ideas.

01:45:06.840 --> 01:45:10.080
So I [? appreciate. ?]

01:45:10.080 --> 01:45:13.710
And that actually kind of brings
me to sort of a higher level

01:45:13.710 --> 01:45:18.630
question that I thought
maybe you could end with,

01:45:18.630 --> 01:45:20.400
because we're pretty
close to out of time.

01:45:20.400 --> 01:45:25.470
But you've taken us through
your whole life's journey, which

01:45:25.470 --> 01:45:30.690
was amazing to see,
actually, how it all started

01:45:30.690 --> 01:45:32.370
and how one thing
led to the other,

01:45:32.370 --> 01:45:34.170
and this idea triggered
something that

01:45:34.170 --> 01:45:36.550
happened to you years later.

01:45:36.550 --> 01:45:40.860
And I love how there's an
overall overarching arc

01:45:40.860 --> 01:45:44.880
of creativity and design.

01:45:44.880 --> 01:45:46.590
And also there's a
lot of creativity

01:45:46.590 --> 01:45:49.950
in the small with your
thinking about how

01:45:49.950 --> 01:45:52.120
to be creative on a
second-to-second basis

01:45:52.120 --> 01:45:54.030
with what we just saw here.

01:45:54.030 --> 01:45:57.750
But my question really is, what
happens when you get stuck?

01:45:57.750 --> 01:46:00.250
Like, does that happen to you?

01:46:00.250 --> 01:46:03.950
Do you ever have writer's block,
or coder's block or something

01:46:03.950 --> 01:46:04.450
like that?

01:46:04.450 --> 01:46:06.240
And if so, how do
you get past it

01:46:06.240 --> 01:46:10.230
and what is your
creative process

01:46:10.230 --> 01:46:12.420
for coming up with new ideas?

01:46:12.420 --> 01:46:14.380
No, my problem is the opposite.

01:46:14.380 --> 01:46:18.810
Like I have too many
ideas, it just never ends.

01:46:18.810 --> 01:46:26.430
And so, these days, I'm trying
to not be creative and just

01:46:26.430 --> 01:46:32.650
work on execution because,
I'll start working on something

01:46:32.650 --> 01:46:34.300
and then I'll think
of something else.

01:46:34.300 --> 01:46:38.500
And I could just keep
coming up with new ideas.

01:46:38.500 --> 01:46:40.630
OK but so, so all right,
so it's awesome you

01:46:40.630 --> 01:46:41.630
don't have that problem.

01:46:41.630 --> 01:46:44.005
Some of us might have that
problem every once in a while,

01:46:44.005 --> 01:46:45.310
like what do I do next.

01:46:45.310 --> 01:46:48.610
So, what would you say,
like what helps you?

01:46:48.610 --> 01:46:51.550
How did you get to the
point where, essentially you

01:46:51.550 --> 01:46:53.440
have too many ideas?

01:46:53.440 --> 01:46:54.160
Oh, I don't know.

01:46:54.160 --> 01:46:55.448
I think I was always like.

01:46:55.448 --> 01:46:55.990
I don't know.

01:47:01.360 --> 01:47:07.642
I mean I just can't, I mean
I get stuck in the subway

01:47:07.642 --> 01:47:09.100
and I see a
[? Subway ?] commercial

01:47:09.100 --> 01:47:13.570
and it gives me an
idea or something.

01:47:13.570 --> 01:47:15.230
Write that down
everyone, subway--

01:47:15.230 --> 01:47:18.310
No no no no no no.

01:47:18.310 --> 01:47:21.460
Yeah, I'm really
hesitant to give advice

01:47:21.460 --> 01:47:25.160
because I don't know why
I have all these ideas.

01:47:25.160 --> 01:47:28.420
But, one thing I can
think of, I don't

01:47:28.420 --> 01:47:34.720
know if it's the result,
the ideas, or the symptom

01:47:34.720 --> 01:47:36.370
of the way my mind works.

01:47:36.370 --> 01:47:42.760
But, I kind of never
had a very normal life,

01:47:42.760 --> 01:47:47.200
like I'm constantly changing.

01:47:47.200 --> 01:47:50.080
I freelance, I work
lots of contracts,

01:47:50.080 --> 01:47:56.345
I'm always changing my
sleep cycle, traveling.

01:47:56.345 --> 01:47:57.220
And your environment.

01:47:57.220 --> 01:47:59.050
Environment, Yeah.

01:47:59.050 --> 01:48:03.700
I'm not one to just stay.

01:48:03.700 --> 01:48:08.020
Like I tried a full time
job about a year ago.

01:48:08.020 --> 01:48:12.320
I did one year and I
just couldn't do it.

01:48:12.320 --> 01:48:13.540
So, Yeah, I don't know.

01:48:13.540 --> 01:48:18.770
I don't know if that's
helpful to other people.

01:48:18.770 --> 01:48:22.060
Well just to my take on it from
looking at what you've done.

01:48:22.060 --> 01:48:23.740
I think maybe one
way to interpret

01:48:23.740 --> 01:48:27.340
it is that, you have done
a lot of different things

01:48:27.340 --> 01:48:28.720
and had a lot of experiences.

01:48:28.720 --> 01:48:31.300
But the way I think,
or I guess the way

01:48:31.300 --> 01:48:34.750
I think Jason's mind works
is that, he essentially uses

01:48:34.750 --> 01:48:38.750
every slight, little
opportunity that comes his way,

01:48:38.750 --> 01:48:41.530
whether it's what he had
for breakfast or just

01:48:41.530 --> 01:48:44.090
a blinking sign on the road.

01:48:44.090 --> 01:48:45.227
Especially blinking signs.

01:48:45.227 --> 01:48:46.810
Especially blinking
signs on the road.

01:48:46.810 --> 01:48:50.560
But sometimes what you had
for breakfast as a way of,

01:48:50.560 --> 01:48:53.680
everything is input
and everything

01:48:53.680 --> 01:48:55.150
is sort of is going in.

01:48:55.150 --> 01:48:59.140
And changing your
environment a lot

01:48:59.140 --> 01:49:01.142
and trying all these
different things I

01:49:01.142 --> 01:49:02.350
think all kind of contribute.

01:49:02.350 --> 01:49:03.975
But what I see
happening is that, there

01:49:03.975 --> 01:49:07.180
is this wonderful
feedback cycle happening

01:49:07.180 --> 01:49:10.750
throughout your journey
where something happens here,

01:49:10.750 --> 01:49:12.355
and then how to use it later.

01:49:12.355 --> 01:49:14.230
And then that causes
something else to happen

01:49:14.230 --> 01:49:15.190
and then you know
how to use it later.

01:49:15.190 --> 01:49:16.900
And I think all those
things essentially

01:49:16.900 --> 01:49:21.970
help feed on each other to,
essentially make this happen.

01:49:21.970 --> 01:49:24.460
You know what, you just
made me think of something.

01:49:24.460 --> 01:49:28.210
Something I did notice about
myself that I don't really

01:49:28.210 --> 01:49:33.010
see other people doing, is
that I give a lot of attention

01:49:33.010 --> 01:49:35.860
to unimportant details.

01:49:35.860 --> 01:49:37.525
Unimportant details?

01:49:37.525 --> 01:49:38.900
Attention to
unimportant details.

01:49:38.900 --> 01:49:43.330
Yeah, like texture
on the floor or how

01:49:43.330 --> 01:49:46.690
light reflects somewhere,
these kinds of things.

01:49:46.690 --> 01:49:49.240
Or, the sound as
you're walking down

